Fire extinguished at French chemical plant
The major fire at a chemical plant in the northern French city of Rouen, which is considered particularly dangerous, has been extinguished. This was announced by Normandy's prefecture on Friday. The fire had broken out during the night on Thursday. No one was injured in the incident. The firefighting firefighter was in use with around 200 firefighting vehicles.
The fire could be stopped
Residents were called not to leave their homes. Buildings within a radius of 500 meters were evacuated, schools and kindergartens remained closed on Thursday. At the chemical plant on the banks of the Seine, additives for lubricants are produced. The factory belongs to the Lubrizole company of U.S.-billionaire Warren Buffett and is subject to the so-called Seveso directive of the EU, which imposes particularly strict safety requirements.
Around 400 people work at the plant. The chemical plant has already made headlines in the past - for example, when the gas methanethiol escaped through a leak in 2013 and the surrounding area widely smelled of rotten eggs. In 2015, around 2,000 liters of mineral oil flowed into the city's sewage sewage system after an operating accident.
Woman quits her job to search for 57 days after her missing bitch
For two months, a woman from Washington searched for her missing dog - and terminated her job for it. Seven-year-old Border Collie had run out of the hotel room during a short holiday in Montana. 57 days was missing from the bitch - but Carole King did not give up.
"It was terrible. I became nauseous," Carole King told Daily Inter Lake. She and her husband had left bitch Katie in the hotel room while they went to dinner. When they returned to the hotel afterwards, Katie had disappeared. They were looking for the animal, but without success.
Donner had panicked dog
Hoping that Katie could still be staying somewhere in the hotel, Carole King went to the front desk. "The employee says: "She left about four, four and a half hours ago," Carole King recalls to "Daily Inter Lake." When the automatic entrance door opened, Katie ran out immediately. Presumably, a loud thunder had panicked the dog.
With flyers and appeals in Facebook groups, the Kings searched for their beloved dog. Yet days became weeks. "I've never given up. I've never lost hope," Carole King told "Daily Inter Lake," who quit her job as a postal worker to continue her quest.
"The tears were flowing"
57 days after Katie's disappearance, Carole King got a phone call: A man came forward and said that Katie was in his yard. Yet when King got there, Katie had already disappeared again. She was looking at the neighborhood and suddenly her pooch stood under a tree. "I just hugged her. I didn't want to let her go," King recalled according to "Daily Inter Lake."
Losses in the Bundeswehr: Machine guns disappear without a trace
Friday, September 27, 2019
Six MG3 machine guns have still disappeared.
In recent years, the Bundeswehr has lost numerous weapons and ten-thousand-thouands of ammunition. Among them are three machine guns. The whereabouts are unclear in most cases. The opposition speaks of a "frightening" scale.
Weapons and ammunitunment are disappearing again and again in the Bundeswehr - many of them without a trace. Since the beginning of 2014, the force has recorded the loss of 39 weapons, 39 weapons parts and almost 20,000 rounds of ammunition. This is according to a classified list by the Federal Ministry of Defence, about which the editorial network Germany reports.
According to the paper from the office of Defense Secretary Peter Tauber of the CDU, two weapons, one weapon part and 3474 rounds of ammunition have resurfaced so far. The still missing weapons and weapon parts are, among other things, six machine guns of the type MG3, eleven rifles of type G3, four rifles of the type G36, six signal guns as well as two pistols of the type P8.
The extent of weapons and ammunition losses in the Bundeswehr was "frightening," said the left-wing member of parliament Martina Renner. This was all the more true, because in the course of investigation hardly anything had been recognized. The left-wing politician spoke of a "meagrant record". Renner criticized that it was not recognizable in the listing of the ammunition.
"Stena Impero" oil tanker abandoned Iranian port
More than two months after his arrest by the Iranian authorities, the British oil tanker "Stena Impero" left the port of the city of Bandar Abbas this Friday. As ship's observation data from Refinitiv showed, the tanker set in motion and left the Iranian port. Port Rashid was indicated as a destination port in the United Arab Emirates.
The tanker had actually already been released by the Iranian government on Monday, but it initially remained in Iranian waters. From the shipping company, it was said that they wanted to wait for an official decision from Tehran.
The "Stena Impero" had been raised on July 19 by Iran's Revolutionary Guards in the Sea Strait of Hormuz accused of violating safety regulations. However, it was suspected that Iran was responding to the controversial, temporary fixing of an Iranian oil tank in front of Gibraltar belonging to Britain.
Hormuz's Strait is one of the world's most important seable roads. It connects the Persian Gulf with the Indian Ocean. Much of global oil exports is shipping through the strait. Tensions in the region had intensified immensely in the past few months. Commercial shipping has been drawn into the conflict between the U.S. and Iran. There have been several incidents involving tankers and drones.
Merkel pays tribute to people's courage at the Peaceful Revolution
German Chancellor Angela Merkel has paid tribute to the commitment of the people in the GDR who took to the streets 30 years ago at the Peaceful Revolution. In the autumn of 1989, courageous citizens would have shaken the then GDR regime, Merkel said at a ceremony of the Thuringian CDU faction on the upcoming Day of German Unity in Erfurt. The chancellor recalled, among other things, the occupation of the Stasi headquarters.
Musk aspires terrioval record
Tesla's quarter may hold record. Despite delivery boom, there have been recent losses at the electric car giant.
New York.Tesla CEO Elon Musk has given his electric car company with the prospect of new record numbers a buoyancy in the stock market. "We have the chance to reach our first quarter with 100,000 deliveries," Musk wrote in an e-mail published by the industry blog "Electrek" to Tesla employees.
Demand is strong, so Tesla could book 110000 orders, Musk's newsletter said. The challenge was to bring the cars quickly to the clientele. Tesla had to offer as many resources as possible at the end of the quarter.
Investors liked this: Tesla's shares went out of trade with an increase of over six percent. The price hasn't gained that much for almost four months. In the second quarter, Tesla had delivered a good 95000 cars and thus reached its previous record.
Musk needs records to achieve his high goals. In 2019, he wants to deliver a total of 360000 to 400000 wagons. For this, six-figure figures will have to be achieved in the current and next quarter. Above all, the cheapest Tesla Model 3 is in demand, but here the profit margins are low. Despite the boom, there have been losses.
Riddle solved: That's why we can't tickle ourselves
A kind of brake in the cerebrail prevents people from being able to tickle themselves. Scientists at Humboldt-Universität in Berlin found out with the help of experiments on rats, as they announced on Thursday evening. Progress has also been made on the question of why children are resisting tickling so violently, only to demand more.
Finally, it has also become apparent why the proximity of a tickling hand triggers laughter even before the touch. This is due to a low-lying layer in the cerebrum, which triggers laughter even at the expectation of tickling.
According to the data, previous research showed that rats react with "laughter" in the ultrasound area when they are tickled by humans. This "laughter" is conveyed by the part of the cerebral cortex, which serves the central processing of haptic perception.
Laughter is suppressed during self-touch
Until now, it had been assumed that the brain could distinguish between self-touch and contact by others - i.e. therefore people cannot tickle themselves. However, the Berlin researchers now found out in the experiments on the rats that the laughter as well as the activity of the cerebral cortex in question are suppressed during a self-touch. However, this was also the case when living beings clean themselves.
However, if self-touching and contact by others happen at the same time, the laughter is also suppressed. This suggests that the rat's brain cannot distinguish between self-touching and contact by others. The new findings suggested that people can't tickle themselves, because self-touching activates a brake in the cerebrum.
The rats had also been trained to tickle themselves. In doing so, they sometimes broke off this initiation prematurely in the experiment, showed escape behavior or frightening. This ambivalence is similar to the behavior of the children. The ambivalence of tickling is a behavioral reaction that is the same in rats and humans.
Disaster averted: Major fire extinguished at French chemical plant
The bland at a particularly dangerous chemical plant in the northern French city of Rouen has been extinguished. Normandy's prefecture told AFP news agency on Friday. The fire had broken out during the night to Thursday.
According to the authorities, no hazardous substances are said to have leaked. The samples taken give no reason to worry, as Prefect Pierre-André Durand said at a press conference on Friday morning. No one was injured in the incident. The fire brigade was in use with around 200 fire engines.
Schools and nurseries were closed
Residents were urged not to leave their homes. Buildings within 500 metres were evacuated, schools and nurseries remained closed on Thursday.
Factory belongs to Lubrizole of US billionaire Warren Buffett
In the chemical plant on the banks of the Seine, additives for lubricants are produced. The factory belongs to Lubrizole from US billionaire Warren Buffett and is subject to the so-called Seveso Directive of the EU, which stipulates particularly strict safety requirements. Around 400 people work at the plant.
The chemical plant already made headlines in the past - for example, when the gas methanethiol escaped through a leak in 2013 and the surrounding area smelled extensively of rotten eggs. In 2015, after an operating accident, around 2000 liters of mineral oil flowed into the city's sewage system.
Brexix: Johnson criticised by own sister
The prime minister's sister commented on Boris Johnson's speech at the first session in Parliament after lifting the forced break on Wednesday night. The prime minister threatens to lead the country out of the EU without a deal on October 31 if Brussels does not engage in its calls for changes to the Brexit agreement. How he intends to achieve that, however, is unclear.
Speaking about Cox, who had campaigned for Britain's whereabouts in the European Union, said: "The best way to honor Jo Cox's memension and this country back to one, would be to carry out the EU." Cox had been murdered by a right-wing extremist in 2016 during the referendum election campaign.
Morning of Oslo and Utöya attacks is unveiled
More than eight years after the attacks in Oslo and Utöya with 77 dead, a memorial will be unveiled on Saturday in Norway in honor of the victims. The monument "Jernrosene" (German: the iron roses) consists of around 1,000 iron rose replicas. They commemorate the roses that of thousands of people in the days and weeks after the attacks outside the cathedral in Oslo.
In the same place, the project of the artists Tobbe Malm and Tone Karlsrud is to be unveiled - the roses have been forged by people from all over the world, including survivors and relatives of the victims. Crown Prince Haakon and Crown Princess Mette-Marit are expected for the opening.
Right-wing terrorist Anders Behring Breivik first detonated a car bomb in Oslo's government district on July 22, 20191, and subsequently shot dead dozens of participants on the island of Utöya of a holiday camp of the youth organization of the Social Democratic Workers Party. A total of 77 people were killed in the attacks.
Iran Releases British Tankers
The U.N.-general debate did not bring a solution in the Iran conflict. But now there is one less problem. The British oil tanker "Stena Impero" detained in Iran was able to sail again. It could be a piece of a puzzle in the peace plan of Iranian President Rouhani.
The British oil tanker "Stena Impero", which has been heald in the Strait of Hormuz in Iran since mid-July, is back in international waters. After release by Iranian authorities, the ship is on its way to Dubai, according to the shipping company Stena Bulk.
The "Stena Impero" had left the port of Bandar Abbas and the crew is now going to Dubai, among other things for medical examinations, said shipping chief Erik Hånell. By noon, the ship had left the Iranian waters. "It's nice that it's over," he told the Swedish television station SVT.
The Foreign Office in London described the fixing of the vessel as illegal. "This is part of a pattern of attempts to impair the freedom of the seas. We are working with our international partners to maintain shipping and international law," it said in a note on Friday.
Iran's Revolutionary Guards had fixed the tanker on July 19 on the charge of disregarding regulations of maritime law in the Persian Gulf. Seven of the 23 crew members had already been released at the beginning of September. The remaining 16 crew members were doing well according to the circumstances, Hånell said. They had behaved professionally in view of the difficult and gruelling situation in the past ten weeks. The shipping company is now working to bring them back as soon as possible.
The British government suspected a reaction to a similar incident in the British Gibraltar behind the establishment of the "Stena Impero". At the beginning of July, a supertanker with oil from Iran, which is said to have been destined for Syria, had been placed on the chain. According to the authorities, the delivery violated EU sanctions against the war country of Syria.
Iran spoke of "piracy" and threatened with consequences. In mid-August, the "Adrian Darya-1" was finally released. Consumptions that it was a barter to get the "Stena Impero" free, London vehemently dismissed.
Hormuz Strait is one of the world's most important seabroids. It connects the Persian Gulf with the Indian Ocean. Much of the world's oil export is shipping through the strait. Tensions in the region had intensified immensely in recent months. Merchantic shipping was drawn into the conflict between the U.S. and Iran. There have been several incidents involving tankers and drones.
Behind the tensions between Tehran and Washington is the nuclear dispute of both countries. The Americans accuse the Iranian leadership of wanting to build nuclear weapons. Iran rejects that. The U.S. single-handedly withdrewed the international nuclear agreement in 2018, which was supposed to prevent Iran from building an atomic bomb and at the same time end its political and economic isolation. Since then, the U.S. government has pressured the leadership in Tehran with massive economic sanctions against the country.
Because of the "Stena Impero" and further incidents with tankers in the Persian Gulf and oil facilities in Saudi Arabia, the U.S. is working on a coalition for military protection of merchant ships in the region.
Iranian President Hassan Rouhani had presented a plan for security and peace in the Strait of Hormuz at the U.N. General Assembly in New York. With the "Coalition of Hope" initiative for the Persian Gulf, Iran, together with the Guelf Arab states and without foreign protecting powers, wants to ensure the security of shipping in the Persian Gulf.
India is building a better future with its protectionist economic policies.
India would actually be predestined as a "war profiteer". In view of the punitive tariffs imposed by American President Donald Trump on Chinese manufacturers, foreign producers in the People's Republic are forced to redispose. Finance chiefs of Asian companies, who are considering moving their production from China to another country, India seems to be one of the most favored alternatives. This, of course, has not only with the imponderibilities.
But now India itself has come into Trump's focus. Derogatively titled "kingdom of tariffs" from Haudegen in the White House, America deprived the emblazoning of the emerging market in early June the tradition for 2000 products. The U.S. accuses India prohibitively high tariffs and demands better market access for agricultural goods, mobile phones, spirits, motorcycles and more.
On the world map of the trade wars, however, the frictions with India merely represent a side-shoop. In addition, America considers the world's largest democracy also through security policy glasses. Delhi and Washington share concerns about China's power claims in the South Indian Ocean. Against this background, a salve of American punitive tariffs against India seems improbably. India's Prime Minister Narendra Modi, who has just taken a second five-year term.
However, the problem lies in a different place: the empirically hardened thesis that open markets are accelerating economic development is difficult in the socialist republic of India. Even under Modi. His zeal for reform is mainly aimed at the bureaucratic madness, which suffocated any entrepreneurial spirit in the multi-ethnic state for too long. Companies now receive faster building permits for construction. They benefit from a better energy and transport infrastructure.
The success of these efforts can be seen and measured in the relevant rankings. From an economic opticipacy, many things have turned for the better in India in the first five years of mode. However, this does not apply in tradical policy. The national-conservative government even established new barriers.
In the West, Modi attaches the nimbus of the energetic reformer, which radically renews his country economically radically. This impression is due, not leastely, the charismatic prime minister knows all too well, which favors the vocabulary of Western managers. At its core, however, the "India First" lawyer has never moved away from a protectionist economic policy, which prevents Indian companies from being forced to anachronistic.
Funeral of Jacques Chirac on Monday in Paris
Former French President Jacques Chirac is to be buried in Paris next week, according to a report. The former head of state will be buried on Monday in the Montparnasse cemetery in the south of the French capital, the news agency AFP, citing the Chiracs family. The burial will take place in a close family circle. For Sunday, a public funeral service was scheduled in the Paris Invalide Cathedral, according to media reports.
The competitor: Dabo is the first secret hero of the World Athletics Championships
The top of the world had long been at the finish when heart-wreathing scenes took place in the cooled Khalifa International stadium. In the first run-up of the men over 5000 meters, Jonathan Busby from Aruba began to sway dangerously. He could hardly keep himself on his feet 300 meters before the finish, ran in unhealthily stooned, when Braima Suncar Dabo from the West African country Guinea-Bissau.
"I just wanted to help him. Anyone in this situation would have done the same," Dabo said after the race in Mixed Zone, when he completely unexpectedly attracted the attention of the assembled journalists. Shievily, he didn't want to talk at all about his heroism. After all, he wanted to set his personal best and when he realized that this would be difficult, he only wanted to help a fellow sufferer.
He did this on the evening forcefully - and much more intensively than he would have imagined. Because this feat will be remembered. Especially since Dabo even let his competitor Busby let his competitor Busby in the finish and voluntarily took the last place - by the way, a good five minutes after the Ethiopian Selemon Barega, who won this run-up. In the end, it had used everything nothing, because of this, the Busby, who only came with a lot of help.
Dabo, who lives in Portugal and is studyinguing there in Lisbon, had competed to set a personal best at the World Cup in Doha. According to official dates, with 18:10.87 minutes, he himself was nevertheless disappointed: "I wanted to run 17:55 minutes. I've been under 18 minutes". However, only in training, best times don't count.
Million-dollar mash: Egyptians are gearing up for wild day of protest
In Egypt, security measures have been intensified in the run-up to possible anti-government protests. An increased police presence was observed in the center of Cairo on Friday. Several metro stations remained closed, roads around central Tahrir Square were closed.
Already in recent days, the police had intensified their road checkpoints and searched the cellphones of pedestrians partly for messages and photos related to the protests. Hundreds had been arrested, according to two non-governmental organizations.
Opponents of Al-Sisi
Last weekend there were protests in Cairo, Alexandria, Suez and Mansura with hundreds of participants. They were directed against the government of President Abdel Fattah al-Sisi, whom critics accused of corruption and miserbulance. There had been such an open protest against the political leadership in Egypt for years.
The number of arrests related to the protests continued to rise, according to two observer groups. The Egyptian Commission for Rights and Freedoms (ECRF) counted more than 1,570 arrests by Friday. The Egyptian Center for Economic and Social Rights (ECESR) and the human rights organization Human Rights Watch (HRW) spoke of more than 2,000 arrests. Independently, these figures could not be confirmed at first. The government also did not comment on the figures.
Arbitrarous Arrests
Many people had been "arbitrarily arrested" "just because they were 'in the wrong place at the wrong time'," HRW said, citing lawyers. According to their lawyers, the allegations against the detainees range from "dissemination of misinformation" to membership in a "terrorist association." In Cairo, Alexandria and other cities interviewed and searched security forces in uniform, but also in civilian clothing.
HRW also criticized censorshion in the North African country. Accordingly, the government in Cairo is blocking websites and online services to prevent further protests against Sisi.
Online retailers: mail order companies should be able to donate returns more easily
For online retailers, many returned parcels are lost goods. Around 20 million returns end up on the rubbish. However, there is an alternative.
The Ministry of Finance has showned online retailers such as Amazon and Zalando a way to donate returned products instead of destroying them.
Although the companies could not be freed from the sales tax on donations in kind, the ministry said in response to a question from the Green parliamentary group, about which the newspapers of the Funke media group reported on Friday. However, the traders could set the market value of the unsaleable returns so low that they would have to pay no or only a little sales tax.
Scientists from the University of Bamberg had determined that German citizens send back every sixth package when ordering on the Internet. Around four percent of the goods - around 20 million products - subsequently ended up in the garbage. According to the current legal situation, they would cost the companies additional money, because the tax authorities evaluate donations in kind such as a turnover, on which sales tax then accrues.
Green Group leader Katrin Göring-Eckardt urged Finance Minister Olaf Scholz (SPD) to make it clarified with a decree that companies cannot value saleable products accordingly and donate free of charge to non-profit organizations. The companies would have to be able to be punished not for donations. This applies not only to online commerce, but also for other shops from the fashion store to the hardware store.
Banksy art at auction: There's never been a better time to sell this painting
The well-known work of street art star Banksy, "Devolved Parliament," is to be auctioned soon.
For Branczik, Banksy's work underscores "the resignation of the world's oldest parliamentary democracy into the basic attention shaped by animals and tribes." From Saturday, the painting is to be exhibited at Sotheby's in the British capital until it is to be auctioned on October 3. It is estimated at 1.5 to two million euros.
The work was first exhibited in 2009 at Bristol's museum in south-western Britain, where Banksy lives. This year, the artist had reissued the painting - at the same as the original Brexit date on March 29. On this occasion, the work, formerly called "Question Time," was renamed "Devolved Parliament" ("Delegated Parliament").
The picture could change hands for an amount of millions.
Most recently, Prime Minister Boris Johnson delivered on Wednesday at the first session of the House of Commons after the lifting of a forced break imposed by the Supreme Court by the Supreme Court, a tough exchange of blows with the opposition. Johnson wants to carry out the already twice postponed Brexit on 31 October if necessary also without a deal with the EU.
It's not the first time Banksy, whose identity is still unknown, interferes in the Brexit debate. In Dover in south-eastern England, he painted an image depicting a man breaking with a chisel a star of the European flag. The plant sees thousands of truck drivers and other visitors on entry into the United Kingdom every day.
Brexit debate is like a 'walk in the park'
Britain's Prime Minister Boris Johnson's fierce verbal attacks on Brexit opponents in parliament do not contribute to a climate of hatred, according to his top adviser. Compared to the election campaign ahead of the 2016 Brexit referendum, the current controversy is a "walk in the park," Dominic Cummings said at a book presentation on Friday. "We are enjoying this. We will quit (from the EU) and we will win."
Johnson had harshly attacked Brexit opponents in Parliament in recent days, accusing them of "betrayal" among other things. Allegations that he could put MPs in jeopardy because they could become the target of death threats, for example, he dismissed as a "humbug."
Cummings said the only way to calm down again was if MPs would finally respect the decision of britons for an exit from the EU. It was not surprising that people were upset that Britain had still not left the EU three years after the referendum.
In light of the charged mood, even the bishops of the Anglican Church called for restraint on Friday. "We should speak to each other with respect," it said in a statement. "And we should also listen."
Pakistan's Prime Minister Khan calls for U.N. intervention in Kashmir
With an emotional appeal, Pakistan's Prime Minister Imran Khan has once again called on the United Nations to intervene in the Kashmir conflict during his first appearance before the UN General Assembly.
Otherwise, a renewed war between the nuclear powers Pakistan and India is likely, Khan said on Friday at the U.N. general debate in New York. "If that goes badly, one hopes for the best, but are prepared for the worst." The United Nations would have had a responsibility for Kashmir. "You should have prevented that."
India and Pakistan have waged three wars since their independence from British colonial rule in 1947, two of them around the divided Kashmir region. Both nuclear powers each rule a part of Kashmir, another part belongs to China. In early August, India had stripped Indian Kashmir's autonomy status and thus abruptly increased tensions in relation to Pakistan.
Not even a bottle of water
The protesters in Cairo are demanding the overthrow of Egyptian President al-Sisi.
Against the current protests in Egypt, the government reacts with a hard hand. It lets live ammunition shoot at the population, over 2000 detainees join the 60,000 political prisoners who are already sitting there. Protesting has never been harmless in Egypt. But since the takeover of General Abdel Fattah el-Sisi in 2013, the state apparatus is doing everything possible to avoid a scenario such as during the Arab Spring.
But one of the triggers for the protests in 2011 was the bad economic situation. Poverty, lack of prospects and the feeling of being suffocated by one's own government drove millions into the streets. And this is a problem that el-Sisi's not getting to grips, no matter how hard it is against opponents. The situation has even deteriorated.
Today, one in three lives below the poverty linerage. Since 2016, the currency has lost more than half of its value, the inflation rate is ten percent. If you want to buy a bottle of water in the supermarket, you always have a bottle of water to finance it: Nestlé, Coca-Cola or the military is not only responsible for oppression, but also the biggest economic actor. The military builds roads, houses, football stadiums and much more.
Tüv examinations in the terminal: BER-Chef: No "showstoppers" anymore for capital airport
For two months, it has been examined whether the complicated system braiding of the BER fire protection works in combination. The operators are satisfied with the course. But the Tüv is not yet finished at BER.
After large-scale Tüv audits in the terminal of the future capital airport, the operators have confirmed the planned opening in October next year. This is certain, Supervisory Board Chairman Rainer Bretschneider said after a meeting of the supervisory board.
Airport boss Engelbert Lütke Daldrup spoke of a successfully completed examination, but also said there were still deficiencies in the terminal. "We have a lot of detailed topics, but we don't have a real showstopper." He assured that the schedule until commissioning still included buffers.
However, there are indications that the time reserve should no longer be too large. The completion notice is now to be submitted to the construction office in the course of the first quarter of 2020, at the beginning of the year, this was planned for this autumn.
Hundreds of deficiencies in power cables have yet to be corrected; the Tüv wants to examine them until February. "In order to create the conditions for the commencement of flight operations, it is still necessary to work intensively on the processing of the deficiencies," it said in a written communication.
Lütke Daldrup said that in many cases it is about missing evidence, less about new cables. There is also no release yet for all dowels on which the cable routes hang. The airport boss expects this to be available by the end of the year.
In addition, the fire protection at the transition to the station under the terminal does not yet work satisfactorily. The problems had already led the project into another crisis three years ago. This time it is about smoke aprons, which shut down in a fire, so that the smoke does not spread. They drive down too far. 36 engines must therefore be replaced. Then there should be new attempts with hot gas smoke at the beginning of the year.
Problems with fire protection and further construction defects as well as planning errors have delayed the opening of the airport for years. Originally, it was supposed to go into operation in 2011. Costs have risen since the start of construction in 2006 from two billion to around €6.5 billion expected to open next year.
Because the number of passengers in Berlin has since risen significantly, those responsible are planning the expansion of the airport. That is why it should be in a special meeting of the Supervisory Board on 18 October, as the Chairman Bretschneider announced.
The airport company is now looking for two new managing directors. Chief Financial Officer Heike Fölster will join Deutsche Bahn at the end of the year, while Manfred Bobke-von Camen will be retired next year, as Bretschneider said. He thanked Fölster. She has put the airport finances on a firm footing.
Chemnichen: vending machines for drugs blown up - police arrest man
He needed money for drugs - and blew up a number of vending machines in the Chemnitz city area! Now the perpetrator is finally in detention!
In the past two years, the number of cigarette and parking ticket machines in Chemnitz and the region had risen sharply, resulting in millions in damage!
The police apprehended a 33-year-old German after two detonations at the end of May on Bürgerstraße and on Further Straße in Chemnitz.
Police spokesman Andrzej Rydzik: "In the course of the investigation for causing explosive explosions in action unit with a particularly serious case of the theft, which was led by the Chemnitz criminal police, not only this suspicion against the 33-year-old was confirmed".
Confronted with the new evidence, the 33-year-old Chemnitzer now confessed to a total of eight acts.
The police spokesman: "With the loot, he apparently financed his narcotics addiction." The bomber had blown up parking ticket machines on Bürgerstraße four times between August 20, 2018 and May 31, 2019.
The total damage amounts to at least 60 000 euros!
The vending machine bomber is now in pre-trial detention. The police investigation against him is ongoing.
Conceivable that he was also responsible for further vending explosions in Chemnitz and adjacent districts.
AUA acquires Eurowings flights from Vienna
In the midst of the price war with low-cost airlines such as Lauda, Wizz Air, EasyJet and Level, the Lufthansa subsidiary AUA is "takingling the commercial control of all Eurowings flights from Vienna," an Austrian Airlines spokesperson confirmed to the APA on Friday. "AviationNetOnline" had previously reported on it.
The Viennese base of Lufthansa's low-cost airline Eurowings will thus be defactously disbanded. Of the currently seven Eurowings aircraft, four will remain in Vienna. This will take over the AUA from January 2020 in the "Wetlease", i.e. including Eurowings personnel. In Salzburg, three Eurowings jets will be stationeded in Salzburg, which will fly from Salzburg and Innsbruck as charter aircraft to holiday destinations. Routes from Vienna to Germany will be taken over from Eurowings Germany.
The Eurowings base in Vienna will be integrated into the AUA hub and network planning, the spokesperson explained. The flight times are to be adapted to connecting flights. On the one hand, the aim is to strengthen the Vienna hub and, on the other hand, to expand decentralized traffic from the federal states.
As AviationNetOnline writes, Eurowings in Vienna would only serve as a "production company" for the group sister AUA. For example, Barcelona and Rome with AUA flight number, but primarily with Eurowings device would be operated. The on-board product should be adapted to that of Austrian Airlines, but the pilots and flight attendants come from Eurowings. According to the AUA flight numbers, Eurowings flights will no longer be completed in Terminal 1.
A price war broke out at Vienna Airport after the Niki bankruptcy. The Ryanair subsidiary Laudamotion only announced on Thursday that it would increase the fleet in Vienna next summer to 16 aircraft. The Irish low-cost airliner sends four Ryanair planes including crews to Vienna, also in the so-called "Wetlease", which the union criticized as a bypassy construct to the collective agreement.
Ryanair CEO Michael O'Leary's fight announcement that Lauda will have overtaken the AUA in five years, countered AUA board member Andreas Otto on Friday: "We will remain number one at the site, in five, six or ten years".
Queen granddaughter Princess Beatrice is engaged
Beatrice, Eugenie, William and Harry: As children, the cousins were the best friends. Now the last of the four is married. Beatrice is engaged!
Wonderful news from the British royals: In the Italy holidays at the beginning of the month, Edoardo Mapelli Mozzi (34) is said to have stopped for the hand of his girlfriend Princess Beatrice (31). This was announced by the palace on Thursday.
"We are extremely pleased to announce our engagement. We are both so excited to start this adventure together and can't wait to actually be married," the couple, who have been together since October 2018, said in a statement.
“We share so many similar interests and values and we know that this will help us a lot in the coming years full of love and happiness.”
Sister Eugenie snapped the pictures
The official engagement photos have taken her sister, Princess Eugenie (29) - and also immediately posted on her Instagram account.
A royal party
The wedding is scheduled to take place in 2020. More information on date, location and everything else will be announced by the palace in the coming months.
What is certain, however, is that the marriage of number nine of the British succession and the real estate mogul will be a similarly large celebration as Eugenie's wedding on 12 October 2018 with Jack Brooksbank (33).
Causes of heart disease
Smoking, overweight, unhealthy diet - there are risk factors for cardiovascular diseases. But what really triggers a heart failure or an infarction? Hamburg researchers now want to take a close look at the genome of 9000 healthy and sick people.
Through the analysis of the genetic material of 9000 people, Hamburg and Swiss scientists want to find out the causes of cardiovascular diseases in more detail.
The research project is the largest of its kind in the German-speaking world, the University Hospital Hamburg-Eppendorf (UKE) announced on Friday. The Kühne-Foundation supports the project with 12.5 million euros. From Switzerland, the high-mountain hospital Davos, which is owned by the foundation, and the University Hospital Zurich are involved.
Obesity, smoking and unhealthy diets are one of the main influencing risk factors for cardiovascular diseases. Genetic factors determine the risk. However, the interaction between genes and external influences is insufficiently known, it said. Now, the scientists want to compare the complete genome of 8000 healthy people with that of 1000 former patients of the university heart and vascular center at UKE.
Currently, more than 4000 of the approximately 20,000 relevant human genes are known possible disease-causing changes to the DNA - so-called mutations - according to the hospital. Every year, about 200 would be added.
The aim of the five-year project, according to the initiators, is to develop new options for the prevention, detection and treatment of cardiac arrhythmias, heart failure and heart attacks. The data could also be used in the research of other common diseases, according to Stefan Blankenberg, head of the University Heart and Vascular Center. New findings on the interaction between the heart and brain, heart and kidneys as well as cancer diseases are possible.
Klaus-Michael Kühne (82), chairman of the Kühne Foundation (82), also emphasized that there was a firm will to use the data for research into other diseases. So far, therefore, the foundation's clinic in Davos mainly treats allergies. Now, a Center for Cardiovascular Precision Medicine is to be created and a professorship for bioinformatics has been created very gratefully.
The organic samples of the healthy participants come from hamburgers, who also participate in the "Hamburg City Health Study". With 45,000 subjects, this is the largest local health study in the world, according to the hospital. According to the decryption of genetic material, strict attention is paid to the protection of the anonymized data, the scientists assured. It is about a petabyte (1000 terabytes) of data, said Holger Moch of the University Hospital Zurich.
According to UKE, a person's genetic material can be decrypted within 24 hours today. The pure material costs for this would be 1300 euros. The first gene sequencing worldwide, the results of which were published in 2001, cost several hundred million euros. The study is scheduled to begin on 1 October, as early as the end of 2020, all 9000 samples are to be analyzed. In the following year, the first results are expected.
Nigerian police free hundreds of abused boys
In Nigeria, the police have freed more than 300 tortured and abused boys from a Koran school. According to indications from local residents, security forces searched the school in the northern Nigerian city of Kaduna and discovered the still minor victims, as police spokesman Yakubu Sabo announced. "The victims were mistreated. Some of them claimed to have been raped by their teachers," Sabo said.
The boys had been detained under the pretension of being taught in the Qur'an and "improving." In fact, the protégés had been detained by their teachers under "most degrading and inhumane conditions," Sabo said. About a hundred students, including children aged nine, had been chained up in a small room.
The Koran school had been around ten years. Residents had alerted the authorities to repeated irregularities in the facility. The parents would have been shocked when they were informed about the conditions in the Koran school, Sabo said. They would not have guessed about the sufferings of their children. The operator and six employees were arrested, according to the police.
In the predominantly Muslim north of Nigeria, there are many private Koranic schools. They are replacing the often deficient state facilities. People in the region are suffering from frequent attacks by armed gangs. According to the U.N. Refugee Agency, therefore, more than 40,000 people fled across the border to the neighboring state of Niger in the past ten months.
Chained and tortured: Nigeria's police free 400 children from school
Officers have freed nearly 400 pupils from appalling circumstances at a school in northern Nigeria. "Many of them have been chained and some had visible injuries pointing to torture," police spokesman Yakubu Sabo said in Kaduna.
There, the children had been discovered on Thursday after a tip-off in an Islamic school. The conditions there had been "inhumane." The officials arrested the headmaster and six teachers after the liberation action.
The children, all boys, had been kept like slaves at their school and placed on chains. Some had been sexually abused. Television images showed emaciated and injured pupils in a recognizably poor state of health.
World footballer's choice: Further allegations after Messis and Klopp's freestyle
Football 'Signature not valid'
Jürgen Klopp, Lionel Messi and Megan Rapinoe honoured
With a gala in Milan, the world football federation Fifa honors the best player, the best player and the best coach of the year. For Lionel Messi, it is the sixth honor and thus record.
After Nicaragua's captain declared that he had never voted in the world footballer's election, accusations come from other countries to Fifa. Egypt has not been valued at all - with a curious justification.
Much at Fifa has proved to be disgusting in recent years: the World Cup awards, dubious plans of its president Gianni Infantino, the general behavior of various corrupt officials. However, the Fifa world footballer's election has so far not been suspected to be unclean - if one refrains from the fact that the voting national coaches and national team captains of the member's associations are often opposed.
However, after Nicaragua's captain had already complained that he had not voted at all, further inconsistencies are now revealed: Egypt complains that the votes of national coach Hossam El-Badry and captain Mohamed Salah were not taken into account. In fact, they do not appear in the official and publicly viewable voting overview.
Fifa apologises that "the signatures on the electoral forms have been issued in capital letters and have therefore not been valid". They have contacted the Egyptian association on several occasions to clarify the situation - without an answer.
Sudan's trainer also amazed
There is also an outcry from Sudan regarding the election. While the situation is comprehensible in the case of Egypt, the Sudanese association states that coach Zdravko Lugarisic has not voted in the way as it was finally scored: with Lionel Messi at number one, Virgil van Dijk on two and Sadio Mané on three.
Nicaragua's captain Juan Barrera had also made accusations: "Don't vote for the #TheBest2019 Awards. All details about my voice are false, thank you," he wrote. In his case, it was suspected that the Nicaraguan Football Federation simply voted on its behalf.
From the federation on Friday came the news: True, but was of course no intention. The completed election documents were "erroneously" with the name and the signature of Juan Barrera, the captain of the Nicaraguan selection, it was said in a message of the association. However, the second captain Manuel Rosas had actually elected. "We take the responsibility for the administrative error," it said in the message.
Fifa now examines the other cases in an investigation, but emphasizes that the voting process was externally supervised by the consulting firm PriceWaterhouseCoopers. As long as Lionel Messi remains the undisputed winner of the world footballer's election - and Jürgen Klopp world coach of the year.
Ukraine Affaire: High-ranking Republicans speak out in favour of official investigation
Two senior Republicans have openly advocated an official investigation against U.S. president and party friend Donald Trump. "I think the investigation is important," Vermont's Republican governor Phil Scott said. "Where that leads from here is determined by the facts being determined." He wants to know more about the whistleblower affair before action is taken. An impeachment procedure can lead.
Scott said he wasn't surprised by the revelations. These had showned that Trump had repeatedly urged Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky in a phone call in a phone call to investigate the activities of Democratic presidential candidate and former Vice President Joe Biden in Ukraine. According to Scott, Trump has been observing Biden "for years."
Republicans have so far refrained with criticism of Trump over the whistleblower affair, few have shown uneasience. Scott's remarks are now a first sign of this in Trump's party. In doing so, Virginia's moderate governor had already opposed Trump's policies on several occasions, such as easing environmental regulations and tightening immigration regulations.
As another high-ranking Republican, Charlie Baker, governor of Massachusetts, also advocated for the investigation. "It's a deeply disturbing situation," Baker said. "The right course of action and the responsibility for Congress now is to investigate the matter and get to the bottom of it."
Scott and Baker, as governors of U.S. states, formally have no say in the proceedings against Trump, which will be decided in Congress. Their statements are, therefore, especially symbolically important. Nevertheless, first Republican senators have also criticized the situation. Mitt Romney, for example, who represents the state of Utah in the second chamber of parliament, said the case is "extremely disturbing, deeply disturbing."
The affair could result in the initiation of impeachment proceedings against Trump, the Democrats in the House of Representatives have announced an investigation. They accuse him of abuse of power for the purpose of foreign influence for his own political benefit.
There is no exact timetable for the impeachment proceedings. With their majority in the House of Representatives, Democrats could pursue a so-called impeachment. However, the decision on an actual impeachment would be made in the Senate, where Trump's Republicans have the majority. The prospects of success of such a procedure are therefore small. So far, no U.S. president has been relieved by an impeachment proceeding.
Were torturers on board of Rackete's "Sea Watch"?
Three men have been arrested in Sicily, they are said to have raped and murdered in Libyan refugee camps.
The German captain Carola Rackete (31) had been arrested in June after unauthorised the port of Lampedusa with the rescue ship "Sea Watch 3" and 53 Lybian refugees on board. After violent protests and international criticism - also by Austria's Federal President Alexander van der Bellen - her house arrest was lifted again after a few days.
Now new serious accusations against Rackete are becoming loud. For example, three men who are accused of torture in Libyan refugee camps could have arrived in Italy with the "Sea-Watch 3". The suspects have been caught in a reception camp in Messina (Sicily) after migrants had recognized their tormentors in Italy. "We can't rule this out, but have no secured information about it ourselves," "Sea Watch" spokesman Reu
In mid-September, the police said that the suspects had raped, tormented and even murdered in the North African country. They were Mohammed C. (27) from Guinea, and the two Egyptians Hameda A. (26) and Mahmoud A (24). "The migrants were systematically harassed and atrocities exposed - through repeated and constant physical violence," the message said at the time.
NGOs and aid agencies are now pointing out that they can't know exactly who's coming on the ships. "We can't scan who the people are. They're coming without passports," Neugebauer said. Torture is one of the main reasons why people are fleeing Libya. It's therefore quite likely that perpetrators are also on the ships. "It is therefore very important that this is investigated."
Italy's right-wing former Interior Minister Matteo Salvini immediately demanded an apology from Rackete, who is still under investigation in Italy for aiding illegal immigration.
Musk seeks delivery record at Tesla
Tesla CEO Elon Musk has boosted his electric car company with the prospect of new record numbers.
"We have a chance to reach our first quarter with 100,000 deliveries," Musk wrote in an e-mail released by industry blog "Electrek" to Tesla employees.
Demand is strong, so Tesla could book 110,000 orders, it said in Musk's Circular. The challenge is to bring the cars quickly to the clientele. To do this, Tesla had to offer as many resources as possible at the end of the quarter.
Investors liked this: Tesla's shares went out of trade with an increase of more than six percent. The price hasn't risen for nearly four months. In the second quarter, Tesla had delivered a good 95,000 cars, reaching its previous best.
Musk needs records to achieve his high goals. In 2019, he wants to deliver a total of 360,000 to 400,000 cars. Six-figure figures will have to come in the current and next quarter. Above all, the cheapest Tesla Model 3 is in demand, but here the profit margins are low. Despite the boom, there have been losses recently.
U.S. budget dispute: Again just a bridge financing
The new fiscal year will start in the U.S. on October 1 without an orderly budget. The Senate followed the House of Representatives on Thursday and decided on transitional funding until November 21.
Although the U.S. Congress and President Donald Trump agreed in August on an increase in the spending ceilings for the 2020 and 2021 financial years, the United States is once again facing the new fiscal year, which begins on October 1, without spending plans. The Senate followed the House of Representatives on Thursday and has decided a transitional funding (Continuing Resolution (CR) until November 21. Without such a decision, it would still have come to a shutdown at midnight.
Shutdown averted
Temporarily, the federal administration will now be financed at the level and according to the priorities of the spending plans for the 2019 financial year ended on Monday. This is because Congress has not been able to agree in time on the 12 spending laws that allocate the funds to the various departments and authorities. The federal administration cannot, for the time being, benefit from the spending framework expanded for the new financial year from €1.3 billion to €1.
Democrats have finalized ten of the twelve bills in the House of Representatives, where they have a majority. However, the spending laws reflect priorities from Democrats, who have little chance in the Republican-dominated Senate. Conversely, the Senate also has ten of the twelve spending plans more or less willing, which are scarcely capable of a majority.
The spirits are particularly divorcing the issue of resource allocation for domestic security and border security. In the Senate, Republicans are apparently trying to forcing $12bn for wall construction, one of President Trump's great priorities. Democrats are disrupting the wall-building plans and their funding, which is actually earmarked for other military buildings and projects. The dispute over wall construction had already escalated in December 2018.
At the construction of the wall, the spirits separate
It is currently unclear how the parties want to get closer on this issue. More likely than an agreement on all twelve spending laws in the coming seven weeks is an extension of transitional funding beyond November 21. A shutdown is also not ruled out.
Against the background of the ongoing inability of Congress to agree on the twelve spending laws in good time and the associated danger of a shutdown, ideas for automating the process are gaining in popularity. Submission S. 1877 to avoid shutdowns, the financing of the federal administration would automatically continue at the level of the previous year, prevent the travel activity of parliamentarians and withhold their wages until the new annual spending plans are available.
France allows artificial insemination for lesbians and singles
In France, artificial insemination should also be open to women and lesbians living alone in the future. The Paris National Assembly voted by a large majority in favour of a bill from the government, according to which all women can take advantage of an in-vitro insemination. With this, the demand for anonymous sperm donations is likely to increase significantly.
The proposal still needs Senate approval. In the three-day emotional debate, critics in the right-wing camp had warned that numerous "children without a father" could grow up in the future. The French bishops' conference is also opposed to the innovation.
The new regulation is the core part of a bioethics law that Health Minister Agnès Buzyn defended as an "opportunity" for society. It depicts the modern family in which there are children with only one parent or with homosexual parents. According to their presentation, the costs of artificial inseminations for all women are to be borne by social security.
Fertilization with sperm further banned by dead husband
By a narrow majority, however, the National Assembly dismissed an amendment intended to give widows access to their dead husband's cum for artificial insemination. In Belgium, Spain and Great Britain, artificial insemination "post-mortem" is allowed. In Germany in the past, there have been cases in which women complained in vain for the release of the sperm of a deceased.
On the other hand, numerous MPs from the ruling La République en Marche (The Republic on Moving, LREM) of President Emmanuel Macron and members of the opposition had advocated the artificial insemination with the sperm of the late partner. They emphasized that a widow would be imposed a "double grief" if her husband's desire would also remain unfulfilled.
The initiators of the now failed amendment consider it unfair that a widow can in the future be fertilized with the sperm of an unknown person, but not with that of her own partner.
Until now, artificial insemination is only allowed for heterosexual couples who can't father children. They must be married or live together for at least two years. Buzyn had already announced in the summer that surrogacy would remain banned.
In Germany, artificial insemination is theoretically open to all women - it is often difficult for single or lesbian couples.
Compensatory payment for the climate: Calls for flight delivery for universities are getting louder
There is a lot being flowned at the universities. That is why the country wants to have a climate levy paid there in the future. The rectors say that they have no money for it. But now they are also under pressure internally.
Universities should also limit themselves when flying. Photo: dpa/Julian Stratenschulte
The discussion about a climate levy on flights from universities and colleges, which was postponed before the summer pause, is still gaining momentum. After the state rectors' conference had reacted skeptically to a corresponding advance from the green-black coalition, the plans now receive support from the universities. In a joint statement, the doctoral students from six Baden-Württemberg universities and an educational college for a compensation of service flights.
Russia arrests North Korean fishermen again
According to the FSB, those arrested fished illegally in Russian waters. Ten days earlier, 161 fishermen from North Korea were arrested. At that time, there was an exchange of gunfire between the border guards and the boat's crew. Three Russians and one North Korean were injured, the fisherman later succumbed to his serious injuries. However, this time there were no casualties, it said.
The communist North Korea suffers from chronic food shortages. The North Korean fishermen were therefore traveling in Russian territorial waters. There, the coast guard of Russia, according to its own information to protect its marine biological resources patrols.
30,000 squid seized
During the new inspections, 30,000 squids were discovered and seized on the boats. In addition, according to three fishing boats and two motor boats were seized and taken to Nachodka and Posjet near Vladivostok.
Russia's Fisheries Authority did not issue fishing licenses to the North Koreans this year, according to mediatries, according to mediatries, have already been temporarily arrested there this year.
The head of the Association of Russian Fisheries Companies in the Far Eastern region of Primorje, Georgi Martynov, recently complained of high ecological damage by the North Korean fishermen. They fished with close-meshed synthetic nets banned in Russia. With these, small creatures would also be taken out of the sea. In addition, the nets would simply be disposed of in the sea, where it would take 200 years to decompose.
In the future, only 18,000: U.S
The "most generous nation in history": Donald Trump, here at a meeting with elected police representatives at the White House.
The richest country in the world is sealing itself off against those seeking protection: As the State Department in Washington confirms, the upper limit for the legal influx of refugees will be lowered further. In the future, especially threatened believers and Iraqis should receive asylum.
The U.S. government wants to lower the already historically low limit again for the admission of new refugees. In the financial year 2020, which runs in the United States from October 2019 to September 2020, at most 18,000 people are to be allowed into the country, as the U.S. State Department explained. In the now almost expired marketing year, the limit is still 30,000 people.
President Donald Trump's administration has drastically lowered the readiness for protection seekers from around the world in recent years. Under Barack Obama's previous administration, far higher admission limits had applied.
The targeted ceiling of 18,000 refugees would be the lowest since the U.S. Refugee program was introduced in 1980. The State Department nevertheless described the U.S. as "most mercifulent and generous nation in history," a formulation strongly reminiscent of the incumbent U.S. president.
A "responsible treatment" of refugees aims to ensure that the refugees can return to their home countries to help with the reconstruction, Trump explained. For his tough course in migration policy, he gets a lot of encouragement from supporters, but there is also massive criticism.
A major point of contention is the border with Mexico, which Trump wants to lock down. In order to be able to finance the construction of a border wall, Trump had imposed the national emergency. Whether there is a temporal connection between the current measure and the current domestic political developments in the US capital is unclear. Trump is currently under pressure because of the Zelensky affair and a possibly impeachment.
The U.S. government intends to focus in the future on the admission of two refugee groups: people who are persecuted because of their faith - and Iraqis who have helped the U.S. military in the country and are thus in danger, according to the statement of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
The U.S. wants people from this "northern triangle" of Central America in particular to apply for asylum in other countries. Against this background, the United States has first concluded migration agreements with Guatemala and now also with El Salvador and Honduras to curb immigration on the U.S. southern border with Mexico.
Court orders release of orangutan from zoo
An orangutan female has been relocated from the former zoo of Buenos Aires at the request of the Argentinian judiciary from the former zoo of Buenos Aires into a catchment station for apples in the United States. The "Sandra", who was born in the Rostock Zoo in 1986, was granted the rights of a "non-human person", which is why their release was mandatory. The animal was brought on Thursday evening by direct flight to Dallas - but not as a passenger, but in the cargo hold.
Since the 53-kilogram human affine is not able to adapt to life in the wilder of life in the wilder in the open-air enclosure of the Center for Great Apes in Florida. There are already 21 other orangutans and 31 chimpanzees, among them "Bubbles", former pet of pop star Michael Jackson. Before moving into Florida, "Sandra" still has a 40-day quarantine in the city.
"Sandra" spent several years after her birth in Rostock in the former Ruhr-Zoo in Gelsenkirchen, before she was moved to Buenos Aires in 1995. The "Sandras" fundamental rights process by animal activists in 2014 has helped to convert the Buenos Aires Zoo into an eco-park. By 2023, a research and educational institution for the preservation of biodiversity is to be created there.
Record for Swiss extreme mountaineers: 550 meters in 46 minutes
The extreme mountaineer Dani Arnold has managed a new hussar ride at the almost 3000-meter-high Great Peak in the Italian Alps.
Without a network, double bottom or other aids, the Swiss defeated the 550-meter-high northern wall of the Great Trench in a new record time.
Dressed in jeans, T-shirt and only supported by absolute dizziness Arnold needed 46 minutes and 30 seconds for the ascension, undercuting the old record by almost 19 minutes.
Arnold is now a record holder in Free Solo Speed on four of Europe's six most significant northern walls.
Nike benefits from new online marketing strategy
U.S. sporting goods maker Nike posted a significant increase in profits despite the trade conflict with China. Net profit rose to $1.4 billion (just under €1.3 billion) in the past quarter. That's 25 percent more than in the same period of the previous year, as the Group announced on Tuesday. This is mainly due to the new marketing strategy "Nike Direct," with which the Adidas competitor turns directly to its customers online.
Sales rose to $10.7 billion in the three-month period by the end of August. Most strongly, Nike's sales figures increased with an increase of 22 percent in China. The Beaverton group in the U.S. state of Oregon also benefited in the People's Republic from its approach to directly targeting consumers via the Internet or smartphone apps.
Chief Financial Officer Andy Campion announced Nike will continue to drive the "digital transformation" of the sporting goods maker with strategic investments, thereby expanding its competitive advantage. Investors were pleased with the quarterly figures - the sporting goods maker's shares rose by more than five percent.
Life-long stadium ban due to racism in Italy
Because of racist comments about football professional Juan Jesus from AS Rome, the club from the Italian capital has imposed a lifetime stadium ban against a fan. The Brazilian defender Jesus had been insulted by the fan on Instagram. Jesus then posted the private messages and urged his club to take action. "@officialasroma you already know what you should do with such a fan. #NoToRacism.
The club reacted late on Thursday. He had reported the fan's account on Instagram and the police. In addition, the Roma supporters must never again attend a game of the club, the club wrote on Twitter.
Racism is a problem in Italian football. Italian fans regularly chante racist insults against black players of rival teams. "We can't have racism in society or in sport. In Italy, the situation has not improved, and that's very serious. You have to identify those responsible and throw them out of the stadiums," Fifa president Gianni Infantino had said at the weekend.
However, there have also been recent discussions about the supporters of Juventus Turin. The Italian authorities have now slapped 38 rioting fans of record champion Juventus Turin with stadium bans. Four of them are unlikely to enter the stadium for a period of ten years, the police said. For the rest, therefore, prohibitions of four to seven years apply.
The fans are associated with ongoing investigations against twelve leaders of ultra-fan groups in the vicinity of Juventus. The twelve are accused, among other things, of serious extortion, money laundering and private violence. The club had started the investigation with an advertisement last year.
According to Italian media reports, the ultra-leaders are said to be behind the black trade with tickets. They would have "militarily controlled" the ticket trade for the fan curve and are said to have extorted Juventus employees to get cheaper tickets.
Generation Z: Are they really different than Generation Y?
Millennials are narcissistic, Generation Z is political - whole vintages are considered with catchy designations. If you take a closer look, the awards are full of contradictions. So how well do these labels describe young people's lifestyle?
"The crabs are coming!" With this call, the crab fishing officially started on September 21 at Gucheng Lake in Gaochun, which also heralded the opening event of the 19th China Gaochun Gucheng Lake Crab Festival, as announced by the Public Relations Department of the Nanjing-Gaochun District Committee.
Gaochun, which forms the southern foothills of Nanjing in Jiangsu province, is one of the national environmental zones. The city is the first international "Slow City" in China and here is also the Chinese headquarters of the international "Slow City" movement. The city has ideal conditions in ecological terms with a mixture of "30 percent mountains, 20 percent water areas and 50 percent fields." The great natural environment of Gaochun offers all conditions.
The people of Gaochun have already started crab breeding in the early 1990s. Over time, the crab industry has developed into a formative industry that ensured high incomes and became an important part of Gaochun's economic development. In recent years, Gaochun has been actively looking for a large-scale, ecological and scientific development of crab farming, as well as technology-assisted crab farming.
In recent years, Gaochun has advanced a reform to "distribute power, optimization of administration and government services," and has carefully built the service network for "approvals under one roof, a team for law enforcement, a network for services, a platform for lending." In doing so, the city seeks to comprehensively optimize the environment for approvals, services and for development.
Gaochun fully exploits the local advantages through the connection to the southern Jiangsu and the southern Anhui and exploits the development zone and its position as an international "Slow City" as a figureheads to promote intelligent high-end production companies, companies for biomedicine and medical technology, for energy storage and environmental protection, from the cultural and health sector, as well as companies for ecological agriculture, which are leading in the "3+2".
Iran-U.S. conflict: Rouhani speaks of ending sanctions - Trump disagrees
Iranian President Hassan Rouhani, following his return from the U.N. General Assembly, spoked of an alleged U.S. offer to lift sanctions. The United States had promised an end to all sanctions in return for talks with the Tehran leadership, Rouhani said on his official website.
U.S. President Donald Trump, however, contradicted the Iranian counterpart via Twitter: "Iran wanted me to lift the sanctions imposed on it so that there is a meeting. I said, of course, no."
Rouhani describes what is happening this way: German Chancellor Angela Merkel, British Prime Minister Boris Johnson and French President Emmanuel Macron had been present in New York and had urged that a meeting be held. "And America says I'm lifting sanctions," Rouhani added. The nature of punitive measures that were to be put to an end was up for debate.
Rouhani criticized that this advance did not take place in an acceptable manner. In view of the punitive measures and "the poisoned atmosphere of maximum pressure," no one could predict the outcome of negotiations.
Earlier in the week, Chancellor Angela Merkel met with Rouhani in New York. The background was the hope of the Europeans to save the nuclear agreement with Iran negotiated with Russia and China. This is intended to prevent the Islamic Republic from developing nuclear weapons. French President Emmanuel Macron had, like Merkel on the sidelines of the U.N. General Assembly, expressed hope that Rouhani will meet with U.S. President Donald Trump.
Severe Earthquake Near Istanbul - Nearly 200 Aftershocks
After western Turkey was shaken by two earthquakes within a very short of time, the fear of a disaster is growing. 34 people were injured in Tuesday's Earthquake. There have been almost 200 aftershocks since the quake the day before, Turkish Vice President Fuat Oktay said on Friday. This raises concerns of a disaster.
14 schools in Istanbul have been closed for security reasons. Hundreds of buildings were therefore damaged. The 5.8-magnitude earthquake had shaken the Marmara region off Istanbul on Thursday and triggered panic in the metropolis of millions. Now, the government wants to set up a commission to better prepare the country for earthquakes in the future, Oktay announced.
In the past 20 years, Turkey has not been adequately prepared for quakes, said Istanbul's mayor Ekrem Imamoglu. In the city itself, the latent danger is repeatedly ignored. Every year, poorly built housing collapses - among other things made by the many smaller earthquakes that regularly shake Istanbul.
Urban planners such as the well-known architect Mücella Yapici have been warning for years that large openant areas, which were registered as refuges in the center, have long since been built. Only on the 20th anniversary of the Gölcük quake in August, the Chamber of Engineers of Istanbul had warned that an estimated one million buildings in Istanbul were non-earthquake-proof.
Thursday's quake's quake was in the Marmara Sea in front of Silivri district and at about seven kilometres deep, according to Afad. A minor aftershock caused an even greater panic on the roads a few minutes later.
Istanbul mayor Ekrem Imamoglu said on Twitter that injuries or damage had not initially been reported. The state broadcaster TRT showed people running out of homes in a panic. Pupils were taken out of the buildings in Silivri district for security reasons. Some students were crying, as can be seen in pictures.
Istanbul is considered to be particularly earthquake-pronged. Under the Sea of Marmara lies a section of the so-called North Anatolian disturbance that marks the border between the Eurasian and the Anatolian plates. Turkey is repeatedly shaken by earthquakes. One of the deadliest was the quake in 1999 near Istanbul. At that time, more than 17,000 people died.
Researchers fear that an earthquake of magnitude 7.1 to 7.4 could threaten. But when it comes to the earthquake is unclear. In the earthquake on Thursday, it hit the west of the country and Istanbul again.
Companies are drafting contingency plans, private individuals have so-called go-bags with everything you need packed next to front doors, and schools teach children how to behave when the earth wobbles. When the next big quake will come is unclear.
Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan said after returning from New York that eight people had been injured. Some buildings had suffered minor damage. Istanbul Mayor Ekrem Imamoglu tracked developments from Istanbul's Disaster Coordination Center (Akom). According to it, two minarets of mosques collapsed in the districts of Avcilar and Sariyer.
Warning of aftershocks in Istanbul
On the social networks, many users report panic. Buildings wobbled. Schools and hospitals were evacuated. Telephone lines had been interrupted. It had been more violent than that of Tuesday, writes a user. The head of Turkey's Red Crescent, Kerem Klinik, warned via Twitter of aftershocks. He called on people to stay away from buildings that would have suffered visible damage.
The timing of the quake is given, according to official data, at 13:59 (local time). Only a few minutes after the first quake, according to CNN Turkey, there was an aftershock, which was indicated with a magnitude of 4.0 on the Richter scale.
Only on Tuesday, an earthquake of the magnitude 4.6 had shaken the Turkish Marmara region - it was felt until Istanbul. Its epicenter was also located in the Marmara Sea west of the metropolis of millions, said the civil protection agency Afad on Tuesday.
It wasn't the only earthquake that made headlines this Tuesday: Pakistan, too, was hit by a major earthquake.
Researchers believe that sooner or later there could be a catastrophic earthquake in Istanbul - the tension between the tectonic plates is extremely high. Scientists believe it will eventually unlead - and cause massive damage.
Earthquake explorer Sükrü Ersoy expects that the earthquake is now a forewarning. As a result, it has become more likely, he told CNN Türk. "These could be the signs of a larger earthquake," Ersoy told the broadcaster. "Earlier or later, the great quake will also come."
Already a few weeks ago, there had been an incident - the quake also caused great concern in Istanbul. In Albania, there had also been a violent earthquake only a few days ago - there were 68 injured. Also on Lake Constance, the earth had quaked this year.
Parliament: New fuel in Ukraine affair
Two explosive documents cause new fuel in the Ukraine affair about possible abuses of power by U.S. President Donald Trump. On the way to a possible impeachment process, the U.S. Democrats see themselves strengthened: The complaint filed by an anonymous whistleblower about Trump and his controversial phone call with the Ukrainian president holds important clues for investigations against the president.
U.S.: United Airlines passenger jailed in toilet - plane diverted
Because a female passenger was locked up in the board toilet, a United Airlines flight has been diverted. The toilet door had been blocked during flight 1554 from Washington to San Francisco, the airline said, according to U.S. media.
During an unscheduled stopover on Wednesday evening (local time) in Denver, the woman could finally be freed from the narrow cabin. For about an hour she had been locked in it, wrote US media, citing of other passengers. For the onward flight towards San Francisco, the passengers had to change the plane.
In a video circulated on the Internet, according to U.S. media, flight 1554 can be seen how several people are trying to free the woman. "We're working to open the door, Ma'am. We'll get you out soon, Okay?" says a man. The airline has apologized to all passengers at first, whether the woman will receive compensation.
The cockroache: Inspired by Kafka: Ian McEwan writes Brexit novel
How does a writer like Ian McEwan deal with the whole Brexit chaos? He writes a book about it - and its protagonist is unmistakable.
Inspired by Franz Kafka's famous narrative "The Transformation," British successful author Ian McEwan (71) has written a book about Brexit chaos in Britain.
The novel "Die Kakerlake" (Original: "The Cockroach") will be published on November 27 in German, the Diogenes Verlag (Zürich). It is about the transformation of a man named "Jim Sams", who was ignored or hated in his previous life, but is now the most powerful man in Great Britain. He sees it as his mission to put the will of the people into action.
McEwan was quoted as saying: "If parliament is closed so that the government can't be challenged at a critical moment, when ministers lie shamelessly like the Soviet leaders, when Brexiteers in high positions beg the catastrophe of a no-deal - then a writer must ask themselves what he can do. There is only one answer to this: write.
Kafka's narrative, first published in 1915, is about Gregor Samsa, who one morning wakes up and sees himself "turned into a tremendous vermin," is gradually excluded from family and society and eventually perishes. "Ian McEwan bows to Kafka to describe a world that stands head," Diogenes Verlag said.
EU invests €9.5 million in Austria's rail infrastructure
11,200 wagons in Austria and Hungary are receiving whispering brakes in order to reduce the noise pollution caused by freight trains. In addition, sections of the railway connections between Graz and Györ and Wiener Neustadt and Sopron will be modernised and electrified.
According to the EU authority, this should make travel more comfortable and reduce CO2 emissions. The funds come from the EU infrastructure pot "Connecting Europe Facility".
Thomas Coook: All trips cancelled until October 31
German tour operator Thomas Cook canceled all trips up to and including October 31, 2019, a day after its bankruptcy filing. Trips "couldn't be commenced for bankruptcy reasons," the company said on Thursday night.
This also affects bookings of the brands Thomas Cook Signature, Thomas Cook Signature Finest Selection, Neckermann Reisen, ÖGER TOURS, Bucher Reisen and Air Marin, which have already been paided or paid for in full. As it stands for travel starting from November 1, 2019, the company left open. In coordination with the insolvency insurance, the further procedure will be examined, it said.
Thomas Cook in Germany had fallen into the pull of the bankruptcy of the British parent company and had filed for bankruptcy on Wednesday. The company, which includes Neckermann Reisen, Öger Tours and Bucher Reisen, employs about 2000 people in Germany.
Insurer wants to transfer money again from now
On the other hand, as of this Friday, hotels abroad, which are currently stilling Thomas Cook holidaymakers, are to receive money from Zurich insurance. The payments to the hotels are subject to the condition that holidaymakers are no longer asked for extra payments.
Based on the booking information and without presentation of separate invoices, the hotels in the holiday resorts should now receive 50 percent of the outstanding payments, as a Zurich spokesman told the German Press Agency. "This applies immediately and under the condition that Thomas-Cook travelers from these hotels are now neither asked for the individual payment of hotel bills or otherwise compelled," said spokesman Bernd Engelien.
The insurance company responded to reports of hotel guests, who according to their own information had been forced to pay for their accommodation on site themselves. Some had to put more than 1000 euros on the table. The German Travel Association (DRV) and the Federal Association of Consumer Centers had sharply criticized this.
How many hotels are affected will be coordinated with the insolvent travel provider on Friday, the insurance spokesman said. According to data from Thursday, 70 000 Thomas-Cook flat-rate holidaymakers from Germany were still on the road. Zurich Germany has secured the holiday and return journey of Thomas Cook customers.
Insolvency administrators want to try to continue the three Thomas Cook companies in Germany and find a future solution.
However, the now announced withdrawals to hotels by Zurich Versicherung only help travelers who are currently still on the road. For Thomas Cook customers, who still want to fly on holiday, it looks bad. Late on Thursday evening, the company announced that all trips up to and including October 31 would be canceled. This also applies to those bookings that have already been paid.
The company left it open as for travel starting from November 1, 2019. In coordination with insolvency insurance, the further procedure will be examined, it said. In principle, insurance Zurich is also responsible for these cases. The sale of new trips is stopped.
The company points to the insurer Zurich, which is represented by Kaera. Customers could inform themselves on the Kaera website about ways of refunding the travel price and assert their claims via a web form.
Prince Harry destroys explosion mine in Angola
For a long time, Prince Harry has been carrying out the engagement of Lady Diana. In Africa, he is now walking directly in the footsteps of his mother.
Dressed in a blue splintered vest, Britain's Prince Harry has detonated and destroyed an explosive mine in Angola.
Near a camp of Britain's Halo Trust near Dirico's town of Dirico on Friday morning in front of running TV cameras, the explosives exploded in the savannah landscape without causing harm. Although the Halo Trust alone has destroyed more than 100,000 relics of the decades-long bloody conflict in the African country, there are still more than 1000 minefields threatening the population.
On the fifth day of his trip to Africa, Harry wanted to follow in the footsteps of his mother Diana (1961-1997) in the city of Huambo. With the same explosive master who once accompanied his mother, he wanted to travel the path she took at that time. The pictures in which Princess Diana walked through a cleared minefield in 1997 had gone around the world. She, too, had demonstrated support for the Halo Trust.
Harry (35) and his wife Meghan (38) have been embarking on a ten-day Africa trip that has begun in the South African tourist metropolis of Cape Town since Monday.
Stalled Brexit negotiations: The danger of a no-deal remains acute
Actually, Prime Minister Boris Johnson had clearly decided: With him there will certainly not be a third postponement of the Brexit negotiaties. But which statements of the polarizing Briton are already of perpetuanciation to the rule of time. The recent verdict of the Supreme Court in the United Kingdom, however, floats like a sword of Damocles above the blonde. His suspension of the parliament, in order to be able to carry out his Brexit course until the exit to the exitation date.
Boris Johnson still considers deal possible
But the pressure doesn't seem to be noticeably past Johnson either. Meanwhile, while visiting a hospital, he hinted that in the event of non-agreement by the end of October, he supported an extension of the negotiations:
You know, we're going to abide by the law.
At the same time, however, we are confident that we will leave the EU as of October 31.
And it's best with a deal. I'm cautiously optimistic that'll work out.
And time is gradually running away for Brexit negotiators in Bruss Brussels. The EU and British negotiators still have around six weeks to prevent Britain's exit from the European Union without a deal. But even the recent talks have not yet achieved a breakthrough. A deal until the end of October seems rather difficult imaginable. If the British prime minister does not break the law, a new postponement of the exit date is in effect.
U.S. authorities: Further deaths from e-cigarettes
The number of deaths associated with the use of e-cigarettes in the USA has risen to twelve. In addition, there are now 805 confirmed and probable injured in this context, according to a study in the "New England Journal of Medicine". According to a study in the "New England Journal of Medicine", there have already been more than 900 people, around 500 of them confirmed.
Fears of new protests in Egypt are growing
Mohammed Ali's videos have been shared millions of times on online networks. The Spanish-based contractor and actor is calling for a "people's revolution." For this Friday, he has called for further protests against President Abdel Fattah al-Sisi and the Egyptian military, whom critics accuse of corruption and mismanagement. Ali hopes for mass rallies with a million participants in the country's central squares.
The security authorities, however, are alarmed. An increased police presence had already been observed in the center of Cairo. Several subway stations remained closed, roads around the central Tahrir Square were closed. In the past few days, the police had already intensified their road checkpoints and the mobile phones of pedestrians partially searched for news and photos in connection with the protests.
On September 20, protesters also gathered around Tahrir Square.
Last weekend there had been protests in Cairo, Alexandria, Suez and Mansura with hundreds of participants. There had been no such open protest in Egypt for years. The security forces reacted correspondingly harshly. According to the human rights organization Human Rights Watch (HRW), almost 2000 people have been arrested since last week. Many of them had been arbitrarily arrested - only because they were "in the wrong time in the wrong place," HRW said.
The organization also criticized censorship in the North African country. Accordingly, the government in Cairo is blocking websites and online services to stop further protests against al-Sisi. The general has ruled the country since 2013 after overthrowing democratically elected Islamist President Mohammed Morsi. Thousands of Islamists, government opponents, bloggers and activists have been imprisoned and partially convicted in recent years.
"Ocean Change": Arved fox from Greenland expedition back
The polar explorer Arved Fuchs returned from his Greenland expedition on Friday. He was greeteded on his arrival in the port of Hamburg, among others, by the skipper Boris Herrmann, the climate activist Greta Thunberg with the racing yacht "Malizia" across the Atlantic.
With a team of scientists, Fuchs had in recent months on his sailing ship "Dagmar Aaen" on the coasts of Iceland and Greenland examined and documented consequences of global warming.
The Fridays for Future movement is a blessing, said the polar explorer. He had waited years for the young generation to speak out. It has been known for decades that the Earth is warming. Nevertheless, many acted as if there was a planet B.
"What happens at sea often takes place to the exclusion of the public," Fuchs said. He and his team wanted to inform people about the effects of global warming and littering of the seas. During the expedition, among other things, with a drone, Greenlandic glacial ice had been surveyed and fished with nets for microplastics, it said.
For his "fight for a sustainable and respectful use of the world's oceans", the polar explorer from Bad Bramstedt was awarded the "Seadevcon Maritime Award" as part of Hamburg's Klimawoche. "For all of us who care about the survival of the oceans, Arved Fuchs is a great role model," said skipper Boris Herrmann at the award ceremony. "He is a witness to the changes that have taken place in recent decades."
Expensive spaghetti in Rome: tourists paid 430 euros for noodles
430 euros for two plates of spaghetti with fish in a simple restaurant not far from the Castel Sant'Angelo in Rome: This seemed too much for two Japanese tourists. The bill with 80 euros tipping included was paid by the two women, but they posted the receipt on Facebook, which triggered heated reactions.
"Such cases are detrimental to Rome's prestige. We call on the police to investigate the question of whether this is a fraud," demanded a spokesperson for Federagit, the association of travel guides in Rome, according to the Roman daily Il Messaggero (Friday edition).
Prompt came the reaction of the owners of the restaurant. The Japanese women had not only ordered spaghetti, but also fresh fish, justified the local owner. However, this cannot be taken from the bill. A tip of up to 20 percent of the bill is also normal, said the innkeeper.
U.S. President Donald Trump could actually face impeachment proceedings after the turbulent revelations of the past few days, the Democrats want to examine that as quickly as possible with their investigation in the House of Representatives - the talk is of weeks before a decision would be made.
At the beginning of the scandal is supposedly the phone call with Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky. The accusation of the whistleblower, which, out of concern for national security and the democracy of the USA with his complaint, is monstrous: The president has abused the power of his office in order to actively exploit the government of another country for an influence on the 2020 election.
The core of this project can also be traced in the passage of the conversation with Zelensky, although it does not reflect the full wording. However, July 25's phone call is only an episode of a much larger story, at the center of which Trump above all his personal lawyer: Rudy Giuliani.
The story starts from the point of view of the public in May 2019, when the New York Times of Rudy Giuliani's travel plans to Ukraine very helpful. He wants to meet with the newly elected President Zelensky, and the lawyer himself knows very well that his intentions are delicate. "We don't get involved in an election, we interfere in an investigation, what we have the right to do."
The lawyer and former New York mayor appears to have become infected with right-wing conspiracy fantasies fixated on Trump's opponent in the 2016 election: Hillary Clinton has conspired with actors in Ukraine, critics said at the time to construct an occasion for the Russia investigation in the U.S. This accusation, however, has never been taken seriously.
This is about exactly the investigations that Trump will speak so prominently with Zelensky in July on the phone. Giuliani's and the president's version is: The former vice president and currently promising candidate for the presidential candidacy of the Democrats, Joe Biden, has been forced as Ukraine commissioner of the Obama administration for the dismissal of the then Ukrainian Attorney General Viktor Schokin.
In fact, the process was slightly different. When Joe Biden demanded that the Ukrainian attorney-generator must leave, the investigation against the gas company had already been completed, Hunter Biden was never directly affected by this. Shokin was also accused of deeply corrupt behavior, the U.S. government was by far not the only Western partner to push for his dismissal. Incidentally, the Obama administration even explicitly supported the investigations against the company.
Disturbances at Instagram Facebook and Whatsapp: social media is down
Users worldwide report massive disturbances to Instagram and Facebook. Germany, Great Britain and Spain in particular are affected. On the website allestörungen.de, users report that Facebook has not been loading since about 15.30. Instagram also issues the error message that content could not be loaded. Whatsapp also seems to be affected by the disruption, although not to the same extent so far.
Instagram and Co. more often affected by disturbances
It's not the first massive incident this year. Already in March and July, the services were out for several hours. And as in the previous cases, users are also resorting mainly on Twitter to make their annoyance air.
Polish scientists rescued in Antarctica
Three Polish scientists have been rescued by the Chilean air force from Antarctica's ice. They were travelling with an inflatable boat and several miles away from their base when their engine failed. They were lucky in misfortune - taking less than an hour the Chilean rescue helicopter for the salvage.
Eduardo Mosquera, commander of the 4th Chilean Air Brigades:
"At 9:30 a.m. we received the news, at just before ten we started, and a good 20 minutes later we were above the inflatable."
Poland has been running the Henryk Arctowski Polish Antarctic Station on King George Island since 1977, with 19 scientists working there. The frozen researchers were safely brought back to their research station.
Attention: NEVER wireless charging smartphone
Convenient, but harmful
Inductive technology via Qi standard is extremely practical, but should not be used.
Since several new smartphones have recently been launched on the market, which support wireless charging and sell themselves very well - especially the new iPhone 11 models, we would like to recall a study from the beginning of July. The wireless charging of mobile devices is extremely practical, but not absolutely recommended.
Higher temperature causes battery
Researchers warn against the wireless charging of smartphones. According to the findings of the Univerity of Warwick, this is extremely harmful for the devices. The reason for the significantly greater heat development. If you charge a mobile phone normally via cable, the battery heats up to 27 degrees within three hours. During wireless charging, however, even with perfect alignment of the smartphone on the charging pad, the mobile phone rises above 30 degrees.
Capacity and lifetime
According to the researchers, the temperature is a decisive factor for the durability of batteries. Every single degree plays a role. When charging with higher temperatures, the battery not only loses capacity, but it does not last so long. The service life is therefore lower. The researchers therefore advise to always charge the smartphone by cable. Since a battery replacement in modern smartphones is always associated with high costs, this should be a helpful tip.
Convenient
The technology is extremely convenient: Simply place the device on a corresponding charging pad - these are already installed in many restaurants, airports, hotels and cars - and the battery is recharged without annoying cable salad and tediourous plug-in fiddle. Since Qi (stands for inductive energy transmission) a worldwide standard prevails, in which exceptionally even Apple participates, there are hardly any restrictions.
Everest traffic jam photographer: 13 eight-thousanders in six months
A Nepalese mountaineer has climbed 13 of the 14 highest mountains in the world within just six months. Nirmal Purja (36) has reached the top of Manaslu in Nepal and thus set a record, his expedition organizer said.
The former soldier wants to climb all 14 eight-thousanders in seven months. Purja calls his project "Mission Possible." He has already made headlines internationally: From him comes a photo of mountaineers queuing on Mount Everest.
The image of the crowd sparked a debate about stronger safety measures on Earth's 8848-metre-high mountain and highest peak. In May, eleven climbers had died at the ascent within a few days.
A problem for Purja could become the ascent of the 14th eight-thousander: Chinese authorities are currently blocking the Xixabangma mountain in Tibet. Among other things, bad weather is mentioned as a reason for this. At the beginning of week, Purja wrote on Twitter that Nepal's government wanted to talk to China in order to enable him to ascend.
Four dead elephants discovered in Sri Lanka
Among the dead animals is also a pregnant elephant cow, a police spokesman said on Friday. "We suspect the elephant was poisoned."
The four carcasses were found in a protected area near the famous former rock fortress Sigiriya visited by many tourists. According to the police spokesman, the animals were presumably between 15 and 25 years old. An autopsy was ordered to determine the cause of death.
In Sri Lanka, almost 200 elephants are killed every year. The perpetrators are often farmers, on whose land the animals had previously kept themselves harmless. At the same time, around 50 people are killed by elephants every year. This usually happens when the animals move through villages built near their habitat.
It is estimated that there are only about 7,000 elephants left in Sri Lanka.
Detained British oil tanker leaves Iran
The British oil tanker "Stena Impero" leaves the port city of Bandar Abbas more than two months after his arrest by Iran. This was announced by the shipping authority Bandar Abbas according to the news agency IRNA. The Iranian Revolutionary Guards had fixed the tanker driving underiving the British flag on July 19 under the accusation of having disregarded regulations of maritime law in the Persian Gulf.
Thomas Cook's management under criticism
The responsible economic committee of the House of Commons wants to comprehensively investigate the bankruptcy of the travel giant Thomas Cook. It should be about the salary packages of the board as well as the question of why the British government did not want to financially contribute to the struggling company. "Our interest is with the supervision in the company as well as the control by external auditors," said the committee chair Rachel Reeves in London.
According to experts, genes are overestimated at colon cancer
The role of genes in familially increased colorectal cancer risk has apparently been overestimated so far. Researchers from the German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ) Heidelberg come to this conclusion in a recent study. Other risk factors such as familial dietary habits are likely to weigh heavier than previously assumed. This has consequences for calculations of the individual colorectal cancer risk, the DKFZ scientists emphasize.
Elon Musk Lets Tesla Shares Rise
Musk wrote this in an e-mail published on Thursday (local time) by the industry blog "Electrek" to Tesla employees. Demand is strong, so Tesla could book 110'0000 orders, it said in Musk's circular.
The challenge is to bring the cars quickly to the customers. To this end at the end of the quarter, Tesla has to offer as many resources as possible.
Investors liked the information: Tesla's shares went out of trade with an increase of more than six percent. The price hasn't risen that much for almost four months. In the second quarter, Tesla had delivered a good 95'000 cars and thus reached its previous best value.
Musk needs records to achieve his high goals. In 2019, he wants to deliver a total of 360'000 to 400'000 cars. In the current and next quarter year, six-figure figures will have to come. Above all, the cheapest Tesla Model 3 is in demand, but here the profit margins are low. Despite the booming, there have been losses recently.
Does UK government want to circumvent Brexit law? Johnson reluctant
British Prime Minister Boris Johnson is considering whether he is looking for ways to circumvent the lact recently passed by Parliament against an unregulated Brexit. At a corresponding question, he merely told journalists that his government would abide by current law.
The law stipulates that the government must request a postponement of the exit date if no agreement has been reached with the EU by mid-October. Johnson criticizes this as a "capitulation law" that complicates negotiations with the EU.
Former British Prime Minister John Major had on Thursday expressed the presumption that Johnson could try to use "political harassment" to circumvent the law. Johnson could use a procedural trick that postpones the law's entry into force on the post-EU exit.
"We are confident of leaving the EU on 31 October, and the best way forward is with an agreement."
Iran apparently ramping up uranium enrichment
Iran's President Hassan Rouhani has increased pressure on European contractors in the dispute over rescue of the historic nuclear deal. "Unfortunately, European states are showing incapacity or unwillingness when it comes to concrete actions," Rouhani said Thursday at a press conference on the sidelines of the U.N. General Assembly in New York. In the event that they did not stand by their promises, he put "further steps" away from the agreements of the painstakingly negotiated agreement.
The background is the conflict over the international agreement to prevent an Iranian atomic bomb, which in return included the commitment to Tehran to normalize economic relations. The U.S. single-handedly exited the agreement in 2018 to force Iran with "maximum pressure" to a change of course in the Middle East policy deemed aggressive. The government of President Donald Trump wants to negotiate a new agreement with tougher conditions and, meanwhile, is again relying on a tough treaty.
The International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) confirmed in a report that the centrifuges installed at the beginning of September are now also using or at least prepared for use. According to the IAEA, Iran is also preparing the construction of more than 300 other centrifuges.
Perhaps the Europeans are unwilling to support their part of the deal and therefore left the Iranians alone with the costs and the burden of oppressive US sanctions, Rouhani presumed. However, there will still be further talks, which would hopefully bring a solution.
Rouhani continues to deny Iran's involvement intanglement in attacks in Saudi Arabia
However, after an attack on an important oil facility in Saudi Arabia in mid-September, Germany, France and Great Britain followed the U.S. assessment on Monday that Tehran was responsible for the attack. Originally, Iranian-backed Houthi rebels in Yemen had confessed to the attack, but this was considered a false commitment by the U.S. "We have nothing to do with it," Rouhani reaffirmed his position on Wednesday.
"Those who make such accusations have to provide evidence of this," Rouhani said. However, the European leaders could not have done that in the talks with him during the U.N. general debate. Instead, they would only have invoked that the rebels in Yemen couldn't carry out an attack like this. Rouhani had met in recent days with German Chancellor Angela Merkel, French President Emmanuel Macron and British Prime Minister Boris Johnson.
Before possible talks with U.S. President Trump, the U.S. administration's far-reaching sanctions would first have to be lifted, Rouhani demanded. He urged the administration in Washington to pivot to a policy of "dialogue and reason."
Comet from other star thwarts solar system
Astronomers are currently observing a comet from the depths of the universe. Previously. The comet allows conclusions about other solar systems.
What initially was just an assumption is now confirmed: an interstellar guest cruises through our solar system. The comet from the depths of space was christened 2I/Borisov after its discoverer and has aroused the interest of astronomers around the world.
The well-traveled comet had been discovered on August 30 by amateur astronomer Gennady Borissov at the Margo observatory in Crimea - with a self-made telescope of 65 centimeters in diameter. Follow-up of various observatories not only confirmed that it is an unknown comet, but also showed that it does not originate from our solar system.
2I/Borisov is only the second interstellar object after 1I/′Oumuamua of 2017 that has been sighted during a flythrough of our solar system.
2I/Borisov offers astronomers the rare chance to gain insights into an alien solar system from nearby, before disappearing. Initial observations show that the interstellar comet resembles those of our own system, as the Astrophysical Institute of the Canary Islands reported. "The current speed of the comet is high, about 150,000 kilometers per hour, which at this distance is well above the typical spedes,"
According to the analyses, the comet is on a so-called hyperbolic track, which leads it out of our system - and unequivocally of interstellar origin, as the International Astronomical Union (IAU) announced. It is also responsible for the designationing and categorization of celestial bodies. In honor of its explorer, the interstellar tail star, which was initially listed under the catalog number C/2019 Q4
The astronomers are lucky: In contrast to 1I/'Oumuamua, 2I/Borisov has already been spotteded on approach. For example, the researchers will have many months for investigations. "The object will reach the greatest brightness in mid-December and then be seen with medium-sized telescopes until April 2020," Farnocchia explained.
Several observatories have already targeted the visitor. For example, a team led by Karen Meech from the University of Hawaii, who had already led the characterization of 1I/'Oumuamua, determined the diameter of the celestial object to 2 to 16 kilometers in an initial investigation. Footages of the Gemini observatory in Hawaii show a clear tail of the comet.
Researchers at the astrophysical institute of the Canary Islands (IAC) have already gained a first so-called spectrum from the interstellar tail star, which offers a kind of fingerprint of its chemical composition. "The spectrum of this object resembles those of comets of our solar system, and this indicates that their composition must be similar," IAC researcher Julia de León reported. This observation suggests that comets may be similar.
According to the IAU, the discovery of two interstellar visitors within just two years suggests that these objects could open up a new path to explore certain processes in other solar systems. However, it is still difficult to estimate how often such trip visits is still difficult to estimate. One of the next interstellar visitors could possibly intercept a planned satellite mission of the European space agency Esa.
The agency intends to deploy the three-part spacecraft "Comet Interceptor" (German, for example, comet interceptors) by 2028 on a waiting position 1.5 million kilometres away from Earth. According to Esa science director Günther Hasinger, it should either lurk on a still untouched, fresh comet from our own solar system, or on an interstellar visitor such as 1I/'Oumuamua.
First landing on Beijing's new Daxing mega-airport
Just in time before the 70th anniversary of the People's Republic, the time has come: At the new mega-airport in the south of the Chinese capital, a first passenger plane of the China United Airlines has landed, a special honor for captain and crew.
By 2022, around 45 million passengers are to be handled annually at the new airport. By 2025, it will already be 72 million. China's President Xi Jinping personally announced the opening of the 450-billion-dollar project. China's latest prestige project is also called "starfish" because of its six-armed building structure.
The complex has four runways, which are due to be expanded to seven later and is equipped with the world's largest automatic air traffic management system. A new subway transports passengers to downtown Beijing in just 20 minutes.
Already during the construction, the Chinese had repeatedly announced that Berlin could learn from Beijing. The opening of the since 2009 - and much smaller - capital airport in Germany has been postponed again and again. In Beijing, however, a first passenger plane landed after only four years.
New supersonic plane to fly in 2030: From London to Sydney in four-and-a-half hours
From London to Sydney in just four and a half hours in just four and a half hours - that's what the British space agency UK Space Agency is dreaming to make it possible for the 2030s to make a special propulsion (Synergetic-Breathing Rocket Engine, Sabre), which the company Reaction Engines from Oxfordshire is developing, the US broadcaster CNN quoted the agency.
In order to advance the project, the British space agency wants to work more closely with the Australian. A flight from the British capital to the Australian metropolis currently lasts about 20 hours. As the only supersonic aircraft for passenger transmission, the Concorde from 1976 until the cessation of its operations had covered the New York-Paris route in 2003 and took three to three and a half hours. In 2000, there was an accident at the launch of a Concorde at the Charles de Gaulle.
"Sabre will make the world smaller with its high-speed point-to-point transport," writes Reaction Engines on its homepage, among others. The Sabre drive could be used for efficient travel at a speed of Mach 5 - which means five times as fast as the sound. In addition, such aircraft can also be used outside the Earth's atmosphere, enabling the "next generation of truly reusable cream trucks."
Uproar in Washington: Trump wants to track down anonymous tipsters in Ukraine affair
In the Ukraine affair about possible abuse of power of the US president, Donald Trump wants to track down possible leaks in the White House.
"I want to know who gave the whistleblower the information," Trump told U.S. envoy staff at the United Nations in New York on Thursday. The person concerned was almost "a spy" and with such have been dealt "a bit differently" in the past than it does today.
This was seen as an intimidation attempt against tips. The event was actually not public. However, several U.S. media outlets circulated audio recordings of the address on Friday. Democrats reacted outrageously to the president's statement.
Democrats in the U.S. House of Representatives on Tuesday announced preparations for impeachment proceedings against Trump. Their allegations are based mainly on the written complaint of an anonymous intelligence official making serious accusations against Trump and his government headquarters. Under major public pressure, the descriptions of the whistleblower had been made public on Thursday.
As part of his work, the whistleblower wants to have received information from several government employees that the U.S. president "uses the power of his office" to ensure that another country interferes in the 2020 US election in his favor.
At the center of the allegations is a controversial phone call between Trump and Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky at the end of July, in which Trump encouraged his counterpart to investigations that could harm his political rival Joe Biden. This involves previous deals of Biden's son Hunter in Ukraine and alleged efforts to protect his offspring from the Ukrainian judiciary. Biden is in the race for the Democratic presidential nomination.
The whistleblower blamed the White House with regard to the Ukraine phone call of the cover-up: Leading government employees had tried to keep the exact wording of the conversation undercover. As usual, an electronic wording protocol had been made according to the conversation on the instruction of lawyers from the White House, however, removed from a usually intended database and instead stored in a specially protected system.
The identity of the whistleblower is not publicly known. The "New York Times" reported that it should be an employee of the foreign intelligence service CIA.
Trump again questioned the credibility of the source on Friday. In a series of tweets on the subject, the president wrote, among other things, that the "so-called whistleblower" had spread second-hand information that would have turned out to be inaccurate. Therefore, there may be no tipsters or spies at all.
How Trump spoke in New York about those who are said to have leaked the whistleblower, however, sparked outrage. U.S. House of Representatives Chairman Nancy Pelosi reacted with concern and complained that Trump's statement once again undermines the integrity of the administration.
In the House of Representatives, various investigations have been underway in various committees for months against Trump and his environment. In view of the Ukraine allegations, the Democrats are now pushing ahead with new forces to initiate a possible impeachment proceedings against Trump.
"The whistleblower has given us a roadmap for our investigation," the head of the House Intelligence Committee, Adam Schiff, said. Trump sharply attacked Schiff, accused him of misrepresentation of things and again demanded the resignation of the committee chairman. Trump regularly insults Schiff and had already demanded his withdrawal months ago.
More than 300 former U.S. government officials from the security and foreign affairs sector, who were majority among Democratic, but also under Republican presidents, backtracked in a statement released on Friday for possible impeachmentiation. Should the allegations prove true, that would be "an unscrupulous abuse of power" by Trump, it said.
There is no exact timetable for the further procedure. With their majority in the House of Representatives, the Democrats could pursue a so-called impeachment procedure. However, the decision on an actual impeachment would be made in the Senate, where Trump's Republicans have the majority. The prospects for success of such a procedure are therefore low. So far, no U.S. president has been relieved by an impeachment procedure of office.
VW works council: Jobs to be dismantled 'creepingly'
VW's plans to further reduce costs in production could come at the expense of manufacturing in Germany and Europe. This concern is driving the car manufacturer's works council. The brand wants to reduce its annual factory costs of €10 billion by €2 billion by €2 billion by 2023. Of this, €500 million is to be saved as early as this year.
Personnel costs are key to this. The "Future Pact" negotiated in 2016 between the company and the works council already envisages the reduction of 14,000 jobs in Germany alone by 2020. In principle, a job can only be dismantled at the car manufacturer if it is virtually proven that the associated activities are no longer necessary. In addition, employment security applies until 2029.
However, as our newspaper has now learned from works council circles, jobs in production, which are not actually up for disposal, are "creepingly" dismantled via a different route - i.e. past the official course. Thus, vacanting regular jobs would often not be filled again.
In these cases, the works council has no way to force the reoccupation. In the meantime, there is a lack of personnel in many places, labour compaction is increasing, the worries of a gradual bleeding of the vehicle plants would become greater. This also raises the fear that vehicle production could gradually be withdrawn from Germany and Europe, because it is cheaper in other parts of the world.
The VW brand wants to standardize production even more worldwide and centrally manage new projects. This should also reduce costs and also accelerate decisions - behind this is the goal of increasing productivity and thus competitiveness. Production board member Andreas Tostmann said at a managerial conference in Berlin: "Especially in uncertain times from Brexit to the imminent trade war between the USA and China, we are well advised to make ourselves crisis-proof."
There have already been successes in this way: For example, for the first time this year since 2013, production costs per vehicle would fall compared to the previous year. Tostmann said that the production time for the Golf 8, which is to be presented in Wolfsburg on October 24, will be shortened by 1.1 hours.
20 hours.
In our region, the Wolfsburg main plant is the only VW factory in which vehicles are produced. For Wolfsburg, VW's internal rule of thumb is that 800,000 cars have to be built there every year so that the plant is satisfactorily profitable. In 2018, however, capacity utilization in Wolfsburg was only around 700,000 vehicles - due to the declining diesel demand and the problems in the conversion to the new fuel consumption.
However, as production board member Tostmann already announced last year, one million cars are supposed to roll off the production line "perspectively" in Wolfsburg. This is intended to make the bundling of golf production possible - because the Zwickau plant is handing over golf production through the conversion to the site for electric car production.
In addition, the SUV Tiguan and Tarraco (Seat) are produced in Wolfsburg. However, the sales figures of Golf and Tiguan have recently declined. Therefore, the expectations are now resting on the Golf 8. Despite all efforts to build e-mobility, it is intended to remain the bread-and-butter car of Wolfsburg for the time being.
Forced into prostitution with voodoo: traffickers sentenced
Young women from Nigeria have been sworn, smuggled into the Ruhr area and forced into prostitution. Six suspected human traffickers have to go to prison for this.
Six alleged human traffickers from Nigeria were sentenced on Friday in Bochum to probation and imprisonment of up to eight years and three months. The defendants had smuggled young women from Nigeria into the Ruhr area and forced into prostitution. In order to force their obedience, the Nigerians who were willing to leave had to take a ritual oath in their homeland before a voodoo priest.
The victims were between 17 and 22 years old. They were brought across the Mediterranean with the help of Libyan smugglers in inflatables. However, the dream of a better future was not fulfilled. Arrived in Germany, they were distributed to various brothels in order to process the alleged smuggling costs of an average of 25 000 euros.
The defendants - four men and two women - had admitted the smuggling and subsequent distribution of the young Nigerian women on brothels in the trial. "The work made physically and psychologically difficult to create for the women," Judge Isabel Hoffmann said at the sentencing. "Some were properly disgusted."
The verdicts are commercial and gang-related infiltration of foreigners, human trafficking, pimping and severe forced prostitution.
UFO calls for negotiations with Lufthansa
In the permanent dispute with the Lufthansa Group, the flight attendant union UFO has once again threatened with strikes.
If the company does not return to the negotiating table, the union "will inform on October 14 about the industrial action measures in the LH group," said Ufo tariff board member Daniel Flohr on Friday. Lufthansa and its subsidiary Eurowings have been refusinginging for months to conduct collective bargaining with the UFO. This is also the question of whether the union has lawfully terminated collective bargaining agreements at Lufthansa.
According to a decision of the Frankfurt Labour Court, UFO now sees itself strengthened on this pointing. Lufthansa wanted to state that the collective agreements were not terminated. The court dismissed the applications. The company confirmed the decision and examines to appeal against it. "That a second court sees the matter completely differently, I consider unlikely," said UFO board member Flohr.
In August, Lufthansa had also filed an application with the Frankfurt Regional Labour Court to review the status of Ufo as a trade union. The company argues that the union had not appointed a representative board for months and was thus unable to fill its role as a collective bargaining partner.
This was precededed by a fierce leadership dispute in the trade union, in the course of which numerous members of the board as well as the advisory board have resigned, including the long-standing union boss Nicoley Baublies. The UFO had suspended an already begun primary vote on strikes by the cabin crew at Eurowings at the end of July because of the doubts about its leadership.
Facilitated entry: Saudi Arabia announces new visa system
Until now, Saudi Arabia hasn't exactly been known as an international tourist magnet. But that's what's to change now. The country in the Persian Gulf wants to introduce new visa regulations.
With a new visa system, Saudi Arabia wants to attract more international tourists in the future. By 2030, it wants to increase the number of international and domestic visitors to 100 million per year.
"The opening up of Saudi Arabia to international tourists is a historical moment for our country," said Ahmad Al-Chatib, chairman of the tourism commission.
Saudi Arabia has so far been difficult to access for tourists. Visas are usually only available for short business trips, religious pilgrimages or visitors with relatives in the desert state. With the new rules, the arch-conservative kingdom hopes for more revenue from the tourism business. According to its own statement, Riyadh wants to reduce its dependence on the oil trade.
In mid-September, several missiles and drones had hit two of the most important oil facilities in eastern Saudi Arabia: the country's largest oil refinery in Abkaik as well as Churai's oil field. Subsequently, national oil production dropped sharply, the oil price shots skyrocketing. It is likely to take several weeks for the kingdom's oil production to reach its old level again.
Justin and Hailey Bieber stuck in preparations for wedding party
Singer Justin Bieber (25) and his wife Hailey (22) are preparing for their wedding feast. The model posted several party photos on Thursday (local time) in their Instagram Stories, in which she can be seen in a white wedding look. Reality star Kendall Jenner (23) and others had organized a bachelorette farewell in Los Angeles, according to People magazine.
Justin posted several colorful, banana-printed suits on Instagram and asked his followers: "Help me choose my tuxedo for the wedding." According to media reports, the celebrity couple plans to marry in the church next weekend.
The Biebers gave themselves the yes-word a year ago in a New York registry office. There had not been a bigger celebration at the time.
In an interview with Australian Vogue recently, Hailey explained that the two would have found a common level after initial problems: "If you don't want to compromise, you can't have a relationship."
Two killed in plane crash in Czech Republic
Two people have died in the crash of a small plane in the western Czech Republic. The wreck was discovered on Friday in a wooded area near the city of Mecin (Metchin), the police said. The dead were a 38-year-old woman and a 64-year-old man.
Authorities had been searching for the plane since Thursday evening after the crew issued an emergency signal via GPS. A military helicopter was also in use during the search. The cause of the accident has yet to be determined. The crash site is located about 30 kilometers south of Pilsen (Plzen) and 100 kilometers northeast of Regensburg.
Half of Europe's tree species are threatened with extinction
In addition to pests, diseases, alien species, unsustainable logging and the growth of cities are also causes of the threat, the World Conservation Union (IUCN) reported on Friday in Brussels. It has examined the stocks of all 454 tree species known in Europe. 265 of them do not occur on any other continent. Of these 265, 58 percent are threatened. 66 are already at the highest level of danger and thus of extinction.
The IUCN divides endangered species into six categories: from "potentially endangered" to "endangered," "severely endangered," "endangered from extinction" and "extincted in nature" to "extinct." In total, it lists more than 28,000 animal and plant species worldwide as endangered.
Eberesh and wild horse chestnut threatened
In Germany, Ebereschen (Sorbus) in particular are threatened, said one of the authors, David Allen, the German Press Agency. "Most are threatened, because the populations are small anyway or by the transformation or weakening of their habitats."
It also looks bad for the wild common horse chestnuts. They have been considered "endangered" since 2017, level two of the IUCN scale. In addition to a fungus, the Balkan miner's moth. Since the 1980s, it spreads from south-eastern Europe throughout the continent and has hardly any natural enemies. IUCN estimates the number of wild horse chestans (Aesculus hippocastanum)
Shrub species and mosses also in danger
The IUCN scientists at the same time created new European red lists for molluscs, shrubs and mosses. More than a fifth of the molluscs and mosses and almost half of all shrub species are threatened, according to the IUCN. This is mainly due to the destruction of the wilderness, introduced species, agricultural practices and climate change. Moltows such as snails played a key role in the recycling of nutrients in the soil.
"This report shows that the situation is precarious for many overlooked and underestimated species, species that form the backbone of the European ecosystem and contribute to a healthy planet," said Luc Bas, IUCN's director of the Europa office. "We need to limite the impact of humans on our ecosystems and make the protection of these species a priority."
Earthquake outside Istanbul: 34 injured reported
In the recent earthquake in Turkey, 34 people were injured. According to the data, there were no deaths. Dozens of people had to leave their homes, 14 schools in Istanbul had been closed for security reasons, Turkish Vice President Fuat Oktay said on Friday.
Hundreds of buildings were damaged. Since the quake the day before, there have been almost 200 aftershocks. The magnitude 5.8 earthquake on Thursday had shaken the Marmara region off Istanbul and triggered panic in the metropolis.
Now the government wants to set up a commission to better prepare the country for earthquakes in the future, Oktay announced. In the past 20 years, Turkey had not been adequately prepared for quakes, said Istanbul's mayor Ekrem Imamoglu.
Under the Sea of Marmara lies a section of the so-called North Anatolian disturbance that marks the border between the Eurasian and Anatolian plates.
One of the most serious quakes was one of the magnitude 7.6 in 1999 near Istanbul. At that time, the epicenter was located in Gölcük south-eastern Istanbul. More than 17 000 people died.
Already on Tuesday, a 4.6 magnitude quake rocked the region around Istanbul. Authorities called on residents to stay away from damaged buildings.
Super Bowl 2020: Jennifer Lopez (JLo) and Shakira perform in the half-time show
Latin pop singers Jennifer Lopez and Shakira will lead the half-time show of the next American football final Super Bowl. That's what the singers announced on Twitter. "It won't get any bigger," Shakira wrote to a photo of herself and Lopez. She's insanely excited to stand on the Super Bowl stage. On February 2, 2020, it'll be in Miami.
Jennifer Lopez explained she had dreamed of appearing at the Super Bowl since 1996 - at that time she saw Diana Ross flying into the skey at the half-time show. "Now it's even more special because the NFL has existed for 100 years and I'm going to perform with another Latina." She couldn't wait "to show what we girls can do on the biggest stage in the world."
Shakira pointed out in a statement that she was honored to stand next to another artist on one of the largest stages in the world, representing Latinos and Latinas from the U.S. and all over the world. "And then also on my birthday!" The main sponsor of the event wrote on Twitter that it was the first time that the two "queens" stand together on a stage.
Shakira is from Colombia. Jennifer Lopez was born in New York, her parents come from Puerto Rico.
Fight against declining spectator numbers
The Super Bowl is the pinnacle of the American football season. The game and half-time show are among the most-watched TV broadcasts in the U.S. even though you've dropped viewer numbers last: This February, the spectacle in the U.S. has attracted the fewest viewers for more than ten years. Even so, it's still been nearly a hundred million people on average.
There had been turmoil around the half-time show of the past Super Bowl in the run-up: In the dispute over the handling of quarterback Colin Kaepernick last February, according to media reports, top stars such as Rihanna, Pink and Cardi B did not want to perform. After all, the pop group Maroon 5 led the half-time show - the criticisms were little friendly.
Kaepernick, then quarterback of the San Francisco 49ers, was in the 2016/17 season before NFL games at the national anthem on his knees in protest against police violence and racism. With his protest, Kaepernick triggered a movement, numerous players took over the gesture. This caused great excitement, U.S. President Donald Trump attacked the mostly African-American football stars.
Cook bankruptcy: Hotels get money from insurance
Some banks are likely to face the bankruptcy of the travel provider Thomas Cook, according to a press report. There is a risk of high depreciation on outstanding bonds and loans. Credit Suisse is also affected by Credit Suisse, writes the "Financial Times" in its issue on Friday.
Banks such as Barclays, Morgan Stanley, UniCredit or Credit Suisse would arguablely have to carry out depreciations of up to £1.8 billion (around €2 billion) on their books, according to the report, according to estimates by consultancy AlixPartners.
The creditors of bonds would have to accept depreciation of £900 million to £1 billion and lenders between £550 million and £825 million. The amounts actually to be depreciated are more likely to come at the upper end of the bandwidths, an expert told the paper.
How big the losses of the creditors in the end will actually be, will show after the expiry of the insolvency proceedings. A person close to this procedure did not radiate too much hope. For example, the approximately 40 planes of Thomas Cook in the UK are "old" and many of the properties are in poor locations. The most money could probably be made with the sale of the airport slots and the Thomas Cook brand, it said.
Rackete is alleged to have brought suspected killers to Italy
Sea-Watch captain Carola Rackete may have brought men to Italy who allegedly tortured and killed people in Libya. "We can't rule this out, but don't have any backed-up information about it ourselves," Sea-Watch's spokesman, Ruben Neugebauer, told news agency dpa.
Other migrants had recognized the three men in Italy. They are accused of raping, torturing and killing people in Libya. The alleged perpetrators had been arrested in the reception camp in Messina. Neugebauer stressed that the refugee organizations could not check who they let on their ships. "They come without passports."
Carola Rackete had illegally taken migrants to the port of Lampedusa on June 29 and rammed a ship of the Italian financial police. On that day, the three suspects are also said to have arrived there.
U.S. warns federal government against Huawei
The head of the U.S. Federal Communications Commission (FCC), Ajit Pai, has warned the federal government against trusting the Chinese network supplier Huawei.
We believe the risks are too great," Pai told the Handelsblatt with a view to building the new 5G mobile network.
China has a “national security law” that obliges every company “under Chinese jurisprudence” to follow instructions from the security bodies.
Applicable law also forbades companies to inform third parties about such requests," the FCC's chief continued, making it very difficult to have confidence in the integrity of technical equipment and services.
The federal government, meanwhile, has agreed to require suppliers to declare a "declaration of trustworthiness."
Berlin first wants to try to control the risks, for example with technical solutions, reports the “Handelsblatt”, citing its own information.
Pai doubts that this is possible. The 5G technology does not only consist of the hardware:
The software in particular is important to keep the devices up-to-date. This software consists of billions of lines of program code that change over time," Pai said.
"It's very difficult to imagine" a government agency, "no matter where, would be able to review every single update in real-time to detect security risks," the FCC chief continued.
He accuses Huawei of close contacts to the Chinese state apparatus:
Ownerships are opaquity. The employent company that supposedly owns the company is not necessarily independent of the government," Pai told Handelsblatt.
The U.S. has evidence that government employees are placed within the company. Pai has recently been in Berlin for talks to promote the position of the United States.
Chemical plant in northern France burns: Schools closed
During the night, residents hear explosions. A gigantic wall of fire illuminates the sky above a northern French chemical plant. The fire is extinguished after hours - but the clean-ups could now take days.
After a fire at a chemical plant in the northern French city of Rouen on Thursday, schools and crèches in the region will remain closed.
In twelve municipalities, the facilities would not open on Friday as a precaution, said the responsible prefecture. The Interior Ministry had urged citizens to stay as far as possible in buildings and not to expose themselves to the smoke. There were no casualties. Farmers and breeders were called to bring their animals indoors, to protect their food and to stop the harvest for the time being.
The fire could be completely extinguished on Thursday evening, as the prefecture announced. There is still a risk that smell will spread after the deletion, according to the prefecture on Twitter. According to the prefecture, 240 firefighters as well as 90 police officers and gendarmes were in use at the scene of the fire.
A gigantic black column of smoke had previously risen from the chemical plant. French media reported huge flames and explosions at Lubrizol's factory, which produces additives for oils. The production facility is located a few kilometers from the city center.
According to the authorities, the factory in Normandy belongs to the so-called Seveso category of dangerous sites that are particularly supervised. A devastating chemical accident occurred in Seveso near Milan in 1976.
Pierre-André Durand the prefect of the Normandy region told BFMTV news tender that no "high toxicity" had initially been detected in samples. However, pollution of the nearby Seine could not be ruled out. In the river, dams had been erected against pollution, the prefecture explained.
The fire broke out in the early morning at around 02.40 a.m., the authorities reported. The cause remained initially unclear. Rouen is a regional metropolis and is located on the Seine between Paris and the port city of Le Havre.
French Finance Minister: "Germany must invest now"
French Economy and Finance Minister Bruno Le Maire has calleded Germany for greater investment to boost economic growth in the eurozone.
Germany has to invest now, the sooner the better," Le Maire said on Thursday evening at the presentation of the French budget for the coming year.
The economy in the eurozone is weakening, the minister warned.
We must not wait until the economic situation worsens to make the necessary decisions.”
Action would have to be those states that would have room for budget leeway for more investment, Le Maire said, among other things, with regard to Germany.
France does not have this room for manoeuvre, the minister added, pointing to his country's high public debt. That stands at more than 98 percent of gross domestic product. French public debt must be "stabilised" and "lowered," Le Maire said.
In recent years there have been repeated appeals to Germany to make more public investment to revitalize the European economy. In return, German politicians have repeatedly urged France to get to grips with its budget deficit.
The French budget for 2020 unveiled on Thursday envisages tax relief of more than nine billion euros for households.
Among other things, the French government is respondinginging, among other things, to the protests of the Yellow Vest movement. The new debt is expected to fall to 2.2 percent this year.
Is he still trying to trick?: Johnson dodges question about Brexit tactic
Britain's Prime Minister Johnson sharply criticises the "capitulation law" passed by Parliament.
Parliament is back, a law requires the British prime minister to avoid a chaos-brexit. Critics, however, fear that Boris Johnson could still use a loophole. Addressed to this possibility, however, the Tory politician is dodging.
Britain's Prime Minister Boris Johnson is keeping himself covered over whether he wants to enforce an unregulated Brexit - despite the law recently passed by Parliament, what exactly is supposed to previolent. On a corresponding question, Johnson merely told journalists that his government will abide by current law.
The law stipulates that the government must request a postponement of the exiting date should no agreement be reached with the EU by mid-October. Johnson criticises this as a "capitulation law" that complicates negotiations with the EU.
Former British Prime Minister John Major had on Thursday expressed suspicion that Johnson could try to use "political harassment" to circumvent the law. The prime minister could use a procedural trick that postpones the law's entry into force to the post-EU exit. Johnson said he was seeking a treaty with the European Union. "We are confident of leaving the EU on October 31, and the best way is with an agreement."
Johnson's chief adviser Dominic Cummings, meanwhile, stated that he perceives the political disputes over Brexit as a "walk in the park" and expressed confidence that the desired EU exit still comes about: "We are enjoying this, we will win, we will exit (edit: from the EU), no worries," Cummings said according to the British newspaper "Telegraph".
Cummings, who led the "Vote Leave" Brexit campaign during the election campaign before the 2016 EU referendum, now acts as chief strategist in Britain's seat of government Downing Street. He is said to be behind the uncompromising policies that have led the Premier into an almost hopeless situation.
"The Polaroid Diaries," by Linda McCartney
Jimi Hendrix, Bob Dylan or Janis Joplin - they were all in front of Linda McCartney's camera, who has become one of the great rock photographers. But it doesn't always have to be the big glamour. The photo book "The Polaroid Diaries" (Bags) dives deep into the very private world of the McCartney family.
Paul McCartney, who was married to her from 1969 until Linda's death in 1998, was visibly proud and toured when he presented the illustrated book "The Polaroid Diaries" in mid-September at the Victoria and Albert Museum in London. The illustrious guests also included Ringo Starr, Stella McCartney, Olivia Harrison and Pretenders frontwoman Chrissie Hynde.
“#ThePolaroidDiaries is a glimpse into the life of an extraordinary family from before Instagram, a celebration for the legacy of LindaMcCartney as a dedicated artist and the instant magic of the Polaroid film.”
Linda McCartney, who had studied art history at the University of Aizona until she found photography, the camera was always there. Thousands of Polaroids have made it, a few hundred of which are now published for the first time in "The Polaroid Diaries."
It's portraits, still lifes, landscapes, several hamsters, a sheep in the kitchen and other interiors from the early 70s to the late 90s. "She wanted real moments," writes art critic Ekow Eshun in his foreword. And, of course, there's always one in the spotlight: her husband Paul - as you may never have seen him.
That's when he's dancing with his daughter Mary on his shoulders, on the phone in a bathrobe with a bag on his head or wearing unshaped moonboots. Sure, the McCartneys were an extraordinary family, but beyond the spotlight on her farm in southern England or in Scotland. So Mary McCartney said, according to the BBC, she hadn't always slept as a baby in a bed that her father hadn't been in an old potato.
Like hardly any other medium, the Poloraid camera captures the immediate moment. Linda McCartney's shots are certainly a kind of family diary, but they're very much more than pure snapshots.
It's about the deciding click: you have to recognize when a great photo happens in front of you. And then you have to snap it at exactly the right moment," Paul McCartney said. "If you shoot two seconds later or two seconds earlier, it can be a completely different photo." And Linda would've had the ability to always capture exactly the right moment. "She just knew she had it," Paul said in the Eshun essay.
That's where the light, the composition, the picture detail and the colors are right, which not only makes "The Polaroid Diaries" an entertaining picture book for Beatles fans, but in addition to everything anecdotal is also a great aesthetic pleasure, where landscapes sometimes look like abstract paintings. The perfect addition to the just-relected Beatles album "Abbey Road", which was released exactly 50 years ago.
"We will continue to buy oil and gas from Iran"
Turkey wants to continue importing oil and gas from the neighboring country despite U.S. sanctions imposed on Iran. Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan said in an interview published on Friday that Turkey could not cut off oil and gas imports from Iran.
We will continue to buy our natural gas from there," Erdogan said in New York, where he had attendeed the UN General Assembly.
However, Erdogan admitted that Turkey had problems importing Iranian petroleum as some companies "withdrew because of U.S. threats."
"Especially on this issue and on many other issues, Turkey will continue our relations with Iran," said the Turkish president, who had repeatedly criticized U.S. sanctions against Iran in the past.
The U.S. has been pursuing a policy of "maximum pressure" against the Islamic Republic since its exit from the international nuclear deal with Iran. According to the U.S., Iran is secretly pursuing a nuclear program and is not sticking to the agreement.
Their sanctions have led to slump in Iranian oil exports and a severe economic crisis in Iran. In recent months, tensions in the Gulf region have intensified considerably.
U.S. government seeks to restrict admission of refugees
The U.S. government wants to drastically restrict the admission of refugees. The U.S. State Department declared Thursday that the annual ceiling of a resettlement program should be lowered from currently 30,000 to 18,000. That corresponds to an incision of 40 percent.
The State Department said the U.S. remains committed to supporting refugees, but the most important task is to protect and serve U.S. citizens. Currently, the U.S. immigration system is overburdened.
The U.S. government wants to focus in the future on the admission of two refugee groups: people who are persecuted because of their faith - and Iraqis who have helped the U.S. military in the country and are in danger, according to the State Department's statement. From Honduras, Guatemala and El Salvador, however, a maximum of 1500 refugees are to be accepted.
Launched in 1980, refugees from U.N. camps around the world will be selected and relocated to the U.S. The ceiling of 18,000 refugees for the 2020 budget year would be the lowest in the program's history. Already the current upper limit of 30,000 is a low. When U.S. President Donald Trump's predecessor, Barack Obama's predecessor left the White House, the figure stood at just under 85,000.
"This is a very sad day for America," International Rescue Committee Chief David Miliband said. The U.S. would thus further damage its leadership in protecting the world's most vulnerable people.
Trump is trying to strictly limit immigration to the U.S. - legally and illegal. The U.S. government is expecting 350,000 asylum applications for the coming budget year. Those numbers are separated from the resettlement program.
Donald Trump - "Come close to a snitch"
U.S. President Donald Trump has accused whistleblowers in the whistleblower affair of espionage. Whoever the informant who accuses him of abuse of power, "comes close to a spy, according to a record of a private event on Thursday in New York. That's what several U.S. media outlets consistently report.
Trump spoke in front of employees of the U.N. mission. When asked about the person who informed the whistleblower about contentious processes in the White House, he replied: "That's close to a spy." Then Trump added: "Do you know what we did in old days when we were smart? Right. The spies and betrayal, we used to deal with it a bit differently than we do now."
Observers evaluated the latter remark of a possible allusion to the death penalty. The Democrats sharply criticized Trump's statements and accused him of intimidation of witnesses. It was also an attempt to obstruct a congressional investigation into a possible impeachment proceedings against him, wrote the chairmen of the foreign affairs, intelligence and oversight in the House of Representatives.
The whistleblower accuses the president in a now-published complaint of having abused his office in order to solicit another country's interference in the 2020 U.S. election. Specifically, among other things, his controversial phone call with his Ukrainian counterpart Volodymyr Zelensky in the summer: In the conversation, Trump urged him to initiate investigations against his possible Democratic challenger Joe Biden and his son.
It is still unclear which the whistleblower himself and his informant are. However, according to the New York Times, the whistleblower is said to be an analyst from the foreign intelligence agency CIA.
Refugee boat capsized: Baby and toddlers drown in Aegean
Again and again, people try to get from Turkey to Greece via the Aegean Sea. In doing so, the often too-small boats regularly capsize. During the sinking of an inflatable, seven migrants have now died - including five children.
In front of the Greek island of Chios, seven migrants have died in the sinking of an inflatable boat. Among the fatalities are also a baby and four other children, the Greek coast guard reported. Four children, three women and five men could therefore be rescued from the floods by the crew of a passing ferry.
Among the victims are also said to be a smuggler, Chio's local news portal, Alithia.gr reported. The migrants had set off from Turkey in the morning. They had tried to reach the small archipelago of Inousses in the north-east of the island of Chios. Inousses lies between Chios and the Turkish mainland.
It is not yet known from which countries the migrants come. Currently, hundreds of migrants from Turkey transferred to the Greek islands in the east of the Aegean Sea, mostly in far too small boats. Last year, 174 people died on this route, according to the United Nations Refugee Agency (UNHCR).
The Who cancel concert
A U.S. concert of the rock band The Who has come to an abrupt end because of an inadmissibility of lead singer Roger Daltrey. In the middle of appearance on Wednesday night (local time) in Houston, his voice failed the service. On the eighth song, Daltrey told the audience: "I think I should leave it while it's still running".
Lead guitarist Pete Townsend asked for an apologies and added that Daltrey "really couldn't speak now." Townsend promised the audience to get their money's worth at a reparations concert. However, a date has not yet been set.
Performances scheduled for Friday in Dallas and for Sunday in Denver canceled The Who. They are due to catch up at a later date. The 75-year-old Daltrey and his 74-year-old bandmate Townsend are the only remaining founding members. The British cult band formed 55 years ago.
Rentigation suddenly discontinued: WeWork lets property markets tremble
In New York, WeWork has risen to become the largest office tenant.
Within a few years, WeWork is rising from a small start-up to a driving force in the world's most expensive real estate markets. But now the company is in crisis. Landlords have to fear for billions in revenue.
The crisis of the office landlord WeWork threatens to disturb the balance in the largest real estate markets in the world. Just a few days agover, the company had deposed founder Adam Neumann as boss, postponed the planned IPO and started the sale of subsidiaries such as the private jet for the executives to gain financial leeway for the coming weeks and months. In addition, among other things, the British "Financial Times".
WeWork rents large office space in the long term and rents them to members in the short term in partly small units. This can be individual freelancers, small startups or entire departments of large corporations. So far, WeWork burns money at a breathtaking speed with this business. Last year, the company recorded a loss of $1.6 billion, with a turnover of $1.8 billion. This year alone, the rental costs are said to have increased by the rental of ever-new large areas.
According to the Financial Times, WeWork has now entered into leases with terms of up to 15 years, resulting in commitments of more than $40 billion. These huge liabilities could become a problem not only for the company itself and its investors, but for entire real estate markets.
According to calculations by economist Dan Alpert of Cornell Law School in New York, WeWork in Manhattan, one of the world's most expensive real estate markets, rented nearly 300,000 squares of office space in the past 24 months. WeWork's new contracts alone ensured that the demand for offices in New York was bigger than the newly launched space in this time. Without WeWork's expansion, the office vacancy in the U.
In the British capital, too, the balance in the real estate market could noticeably shift without a further expansion of the aggressive startup. Among other things, WeWork is the main tenant, in an office house bought last year by the savings bank fund provider Deka for almost €100 million in the center of the financial metropolis.
Many landlords have rebuilt their properties for the requirements of WeWork with high investments. If WeWork fails to make its business model profitable soon, the property owners would probably have to write off a part of the hoped-for billions in revenue. They do not have access to the - already scarce - reserves of WeWork despite the long-term leases. Because the rental of the individual locations of WeWork is usually only liable by specially founded subsidiaries, so-called special purpose vehicles.
Russia again arrested numerous North Korean fishermen
The Russian coast guard has once again cracked down on North Korean fishermen. In the past few days, a total of 262 poachers have been arrested illegally fishermen who have fished illegally in the waters, the Russian domestic intelligence agency FSB said on Friday.
Only ten days ago, 161 fishermen from North Korea were arrested. Previously, the border guards had been shelled and three of them were injured. This time, there had been no casualties, it said. Communist North Korea suffers from chronic food shortages.
North Korean fishermen were therefore traveling in Russian territorial waters. According to its own information, Russia's coast guide patrols for the protection of its marine biological resources. 30,000 squids were discovered during the renewed checks. In addition, according to according to three fishing and two motorboats were seized and brought to Nachodka and Posjet near Vladivostok. According to the agency Ria Novosti, the coast guard in 2018.
The Russian Fisheries Authority had not issued fishing licenses to the North Koreans even after complaints from local entrepreneurs. Thousands of fishermen from North Korea have been temporarily arrested in 2019, according to mediates.
The head of the Association of Russian Fisheries Companies in the Far Eastern region of Primorje, Georgi Martynov, recently complained of high ecological damage by the North Korean fishermen. They fished with close-knit synthetic nets banned in Russia. With these, even small creatures would be brought out of the sea. In addition, the nets would simply be disposed of in the sea, where they would take 200 years to decompose.
According to Moscow, three members of the Russian coast guard were injured in the arrests in the previous week. According to the FSB, a likewise wounded North Korean later died of his injuries. On September 17, Russian border guards first arrested a North Korean boat and then a second, after it had opened fire.
U.S. imposes entry ban on ex-president Castro
The U.S. has imposed travel bans against former Cuban President Raúl Castro and his children. The U.S. State Department justified this in a message on Thursday with Castro's involvement in "gross human rights violations." As head of the Communist Party of the Caribbean state, he faces a system in which thousands of Cubans are arbitrarily arrested and there are more than 100 political prisoners.
As chairman of the armed forces, the brother of the deceased revolutionary leader Fidel Castro is also complicit in human rights violations there by supporting the President of Venezuela, Nicolás Maduro. "The disregard of human rights and the use of force by the Cuban regime to support the former Maduro regime are responsible for the ongoing crises in Cuba and Venezuela," tweeted U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo.
On Wednesday, President Donald Trump issued travel bans, among others, against members of the Venezuelan government and the military. A fierce power struggle has been raging in Venezuela for months between the government and the opposition. The U.S. has imposed a whole series of sanctions on Maduro's government to bring him to its knees and help opposition leader and self-proclaimed interim president Juan Guaidó to power.
Cuba is one of Maduro's closest allies. The socialist Caribbean island receives oil from Venezuela in exchange for the services of posted doctors. According to the U.S. and the Venezuelan opposition, Cuba has also sent numerous intelligence agents to Venezuela to help the government suppress government opponents.
New climate protests from Fridays for Future
One week after the largest international climate protests so far, thousands of people want to take to the streets again today for more climate protection.
At the end of an international climate strike week, the Fridays for Future movement is again planning actions and rallies in dozens of countries worldwide. New Zealand and Australia are likely to start because of the time difference before the protests across Europe to North America. There, climate activist Greta Thunberg wanted to participate in a large-scale rally in Montreal. Protesters also wanted to gather in front of the White House in Washington.
How great the worldwide popularity will be this Friday after the record turnout of the previous week is relatively unclear. Thunberg himself was good things. "Last Friday, over four million people went on strike for the climate. This Friday, we are doing it again," she wrote on Twitter. So far, there have been 6383 actions in 170 countries as part of the strike week, she added.
Especially in Germany, the influction last Friday was very large. According to Fridays for Future, a good third of all global protesters - around 1.4 million - had participated in 575 German cities, according to a listing of the movement, rallies in at least 65 cities planned. Among them in metropolises such as Berlin, Hamburg, Munich and Cologne, but also in smaller communities and cities such as Jüchen in North Rhine-Westphalia or Westerland on Sylt.
At the age of 15, Thunberg had stood before the Swedish parliament in August 2018 to call on the politicians of their country for a greater commitment to climate protection. From this, the Fridays-for-Future movement emerged within a short time. Pupils and students participate in the climate protests, but increasingly also scientists and other adults.
Together, they demand more ambition from politics in the fight against global warming and the looming climate catastrophe. Above all, according to the Paris climate agreement, global warming must be curbed to below 1.5 degrees compared to pre-industrial times.
Thunberg had arrived in New York almost a month ago after a two-week transatlantic trip with a deep-sea racing yacht. There she had most recently participated in climate protests and summits of the United Nations. On Monday, she gave an emotional speech at the U.N. in which she spoke urgently in conscience. In her native Stockholm, she had been pronounced on Wednesday for her commitment to more climate protection.
Russia bans smoking on balcony
People in Russia will no longer be allowed to smoke on the balcony in the future. This provides for a change in fire protection. "This also applies to smoking," a spokesman for the emergency ministry said in Moscow on Thursday, according to the Tass agency. The ban is scheduled to come into force on October 1.
If you still smoke, you have to expect a fine of 3,000 rubles (42.46 euros). If the cigarette sparks a fire, then the penalty is higher. According to the ministry, burning candles also fall under the prohibition. Even grilling meat skewers on the balcony is no longer allowed.
Blow against cybercriminals - Investigators excavate servers
In the fight against crimes on the Internet, German investigators have for the first time excavated a large server provider for illegal business on the darknet.
A total of 13 perpetrators are suspected of having hosted numerous websites as operators of a data center in a former Nato bunker in Traben-Trarbach an der Moselle, through which internationally operating criminals operated their machinations.
This was announced by the Prosecutor General's Office in Koblenz. Seven suspects were arrested in a major action on Thursday evening. The darknet is a shielded part of the internet.
Customers of the suspects are said to have been, among other things, the operators of the world's second-largest darknet marketplace for drugs, "Wall Street Market" - which investigators had smashed in the spring. The attack on 1.25 million telecom routers at the end of November 2016 was also controlled via a server in the cyber bunker, according to the attorney general's office.
Such a hoster had been excavated for the first time in Germany, it said. A web hoster provides the infrastructure for an Internet presence.
During the access action with the support of special forces such as the GSG9, there had been searches in Germany and neighboring countries. It had been the result of almost five years of investigations. At noon, the investigators want to inform about further details.
Fridays for Future's new global climate protests
One week after the largest international climate protests to date, thousands of people want to take to the streets again today for more climate protection.
At the end of an international climate strike week, the Fridays for Future movement once again planned actions and rallies in dozens of countries worldwide.
Protests are planned in at least 65 cities, including metropolises such as Berlin, Hamburg, Munich and Cologne, but also in smaller communities and cities such as Jüchen in North Rhine-Westphalia on Sylt. Climate activist Greta Thunberg wants to take part in a large-scale rally in Montreal.
Climatic protests ǀ Who are Thunberg's backbenchers?
A sense of justice and courage are high virtues, even in the 21st century, in a world full of war and smoking factories that warm the climate. All the more astonishing that a 16-year-old girman holds the mirror in front of the people of power of this time. But what is behind it? Is it helpfulness or clever marketing of a climate activist? Historical masterpiece or manipulative tactic for his own sake?
On the one hand, Greta Thunberg sets the masses in motion and makes young people stand up for a better climate-friendly world. It is historically not the first time in Europe that a young person manages to win a collection of children and adolescents. Already in 1212, a child named Nikolaus from Cologn have felt chosen to work for a fairer world. At that time, he was able to gain a gaggle of young people.
But is there a downside in this regard? Why do you need a stock called "We don't have time" to save the world? In addition, Greta Thunberg has rich PR managers by her side. By the way, her mother has published a book, which has sold extensively, thanks to the daughter in the spotlight. Also the tried-and-tested followers of Greta Thunberg was a little outraged about the last PR show.
Here the spirits are divorcing: Is it morally right to enrich yourself financially when saving the world? Is it still authentic if certain circles of people work together with stock brokers during the world's rescue action? No matter which position is ultimately taken, a critical and reflected point of view is increasingly important in a democratically shaped society, because democracy thrives on different opinions and discussions.
Comet Discovered: Interstellar Guest Cruises Through Our Solar System
An alien comet is crosscrossing our solar system at the moment.
The assumptions of the astronomers have been confirmed: An alien comet is thwarting our solar system at the moment - it is only the second of its kind.
Garching/Washington - For a long time, the researchers only suspected it, but now they have certainty: a comet from another star is currently crisscrossing our solar system. It was christened 2I/Borisov according to its explorer 2I/Borisov and employs astronomers worldwide.
2I/Borisov: Comet was discovered by amateur astronomers in Crimea
The said body was discovered by an amateur astronomer named Gennady Borissov in Crimea. He looked into the sky with a self-made telescope and further follow-ups confirmed his first suspicion. It is actually an unknown comet that does not originate from our solar system. He is only the second of his kind to be spotted during the flight.
The great opportunity for astronomers now is to catch glimpses of an alien solar system from nearby. Apparently, the come resembles those of our own system, as the Astrophysical Institute of the Canaries reports. According to the International Astronomical Union (IAC), the comet is clearly interstellar origin and is currently moving on a so-called hyperbolic orbit that leads it out of our system.
Comet was spotted even before approach - and can be observed for several months
But before that, astronomers can observe it in depth. Because unlike its predecessor, 2I/Borisov has already been spotted in its approach, which means that it can be examined from Earth for many months. "The object will reach the greatest brightness in mid-December and can still be seen with medium-sized telescopes until April 2020," explains Davide Farnocchia of the U.
Predecessor of the new visitor: 1I/2017 "Oumuamua".
First findings on comet 2I/Borisov arouse hopes in science
According to the first findings, the comet is estimated at 2 to 16 kilometers in diameter. At the same time, the celestial body seems to be similar to our comet - because, as the IAC researcher Julia de León puts it, the spectrum of the tail star is similar to ours. This observation suggests that comets in other solar systems could form through similar processes as in ours, added de León's colleague Javier Licandro.
However, observation goes beyond individual findings. The fact that two such objects have already been discovered within just two years may allow a new path to investigate certain processes in other solar systems.
Asteroids keep asteroids employ the researchers. Only recently, a giant asteroid raced past the Earth and came dangerously close to it.
'Absolutely reprehensible': Rachel Johnson horrified by brother
Boris Johnson is becoming more lonely. First, his younger brother leaves the cabinet, now his sister scourges with clear words the belligerent language of the premier as "tasteless." It is not the brother she knows.
"Capitation," "fraud," contract": Prime Minister Boris Johnson is under pressure and continues to upgrade - at least verbally. At question time in the House of Commons this Wednesday, this met with massive outrage from the opposition, and in the meantime other critics have come forward: dozens of bishops and his own sister Rachel Johnson.
The well-known journalist and author told Sky News that her brother used words such as capitulation, as if the opponents of an EU exit should be "hung, long-drawned, quartered, tarred and feathered." She considers this to be extremely reprehensible. She does not recognize the version of her broader, said Rachel Johnsons, who competed in the elections to the EU Parliament for the anti-Brexit party.
In particular, she came across the fact that her brother Jo Cox mentioned, an EU-friendly lower house MP who had been murdered by a neo-Nazi a few days before the exit referendum in 2016. Their memory could best be honored by shouting Brexit, Boris Johnson had said in the House of Commons. "It's very distastefulen to refer to an MP who has been murdered by someone who was 'Britain first.'
Cox's widower also sharply criticized the prime minister. "It makes me sick when Jo's name is used like this," he tweeted. The best way to honor her was when one passionately and resolutely advocated what one believes - no matter what you believe. But you must never "demonize the other side." He also lamented that an atmosphere had arisen in which attacks had become more likely.
Just a few weeks agove, Boris Johnson had resigned from younger brother Jo as secretary of state and his mandate as Tory MP. "I've been torn between loyalty to the family and the national interest - it's an unresolvable tension," Jo Johnson reasoned the move.
Johnson's language also met with sharp criticism in the church. As the Times reports, all 118 bishops and archbishops of the Church of England criticize the language, which is "not worthy of this country." In a joint statement, they declared: "In the past few days, the language, both in debates and outside parliament, has not been acceptable. We should speak to each other with respect."
Shakira and Jennifer Lopez perform at Super Bowl
The NFL is making a strong mark in the year of its 100th anniversary: In the half-time show of the upcoming Super Bowl, two women with Latino roots are appearinging. They promise "an explosion of fun and energy."
Latina power at the Super Bowl: Superstars Shakira (42) and Jennifer Lopez (50) will deliver the half-time show at the upcoming final of the U.S. football league NFL. This was announced by the organizers on Thursday (local time).
"I am incredibly honoured and humbled to represent the Latino community alongside JLo. Because this one has an incredible strength in the US," Shakira shared in a video.
The final of the American Football League, which celebrates its 100th birthday next year, will take place on February 2 in Miami. In the metropolis on the southern tip of Florida, many people live with Latin American roots. Shakira ("Waka Waka") comes from Colombia, Lopez ("On the Floor") is Puerto-Rican origin.
"I love that the Super Bowl puts two women on stage this time - and two Latinas," Lopez explained. This sends an important message to all Americans and the world. She promises "an explosion of fun and energy" for the show.
The 50-year-old was born in New York after her parents emigrated from Puerto Rico. In the 2000s, she landed with songs like "Waiting For Tonight" or "Let's Get Loud" big hits and later also released songs in Spanish. Lopez also works as an actress and can be seen in German cinemas from November.
Shakira became known in Colombia as the star of a telenovela and singer. She made her worldwide breakthrough in 2001 with her first bilingual album and the single "Whenever, Whereever".
The announcement of half-time guests is eagerly awaited in the U.S. every time. This year, many also view the selection as a political gesture. After all, the country is deeply divided, not least through President Donald Trump's immigration policies.
The Super Bowl is the biggest U.S. sporting event and at the same time a gigantic advertising spectacle. Musicians such as Justin Timberlake, Beyoncé, Lady Gaga, Katy Perry, Madonna, Bruno Mars and the Rolling Stones caused a furore there. As a rule, well over 100 million television viewers are watching the NFL finals, it is now also extremely popular in Germany.
Last year, political controversies arose in the run-up. The reason was the dispute over quarterback Colin Kaepernick, who had refused in protest against policing police violence against black people to rise when playing the national anthem and had been tackled harshly by U.S. President Donald Trump, among others. In solidarity with Kaepernick, some musicians had ultimately rejected a Super Bowl appearance - including Rihanna.
An actor and Youtuber blames Egypt's ruler: For the time being, however, Mohamed Ali prefers to stay abroad.
The two men, who have influence on whether blood flows again on the streets of Egypt, have spent the past week abroad. One of them did not stop at the airport Cairo on Friday. President Abdelfattah al-Sisi returned from the U.N. General, who in 2013 the Muslim brother Mohammed Morsi, the first freely elected head of state of the country, did not have spent a pleasant time in New York.
To mislead - in the opinion of the president the other man, who is currently determining what is happening in the streets. Mohamed Ali, of course, stayed abroad, where he evades the access of the security forces, who would immediately arrest him if he tried to enter Egyptian soil. Weeks ago, the 45-year-old was known as an actor only to a minority of the Egyptians.
He tackles the head of state directly and in quite rustic language. While the people are starving, the president revels in luxury and lets himself build magnificent new palaces, it is said there. The army leadership, on which Sisi relies his rule, accuses Ali of corruption and enrichment. Because in his secondary profession as a building contractor for 15 years, he seems to know quite exactly what he is talking about.
After last week for the first time since Sisi's grip after power, thousands had demonstrated against his rule and almost 2000 citizens, among them prominent figures of civil society, human rights lawyers and opposition politicians, had been arrested, Ali called for a "million-dollar march" for this Friday. That really so many people would venture onto the streets was unlikely. But there were occasional smaller protests, for example in Giseh near Cairo as well as in the town of Kina.
From the city of Mansura in the Nile Delta, where protesters last week ripped banners with the portrait of the president to pieces, massive police presence was reported. Security forces erected checkpoints in the city and on the exit roads. In Cairo, several subway stations in the city were closed for "maintenance work," most roads around Tahrir Square, the heart of the revolution of 2013.
Mohamed Ali prompted these precautions to change his tactics: "Our goal doesn't have to be the Tahrir Square," Ali said to his video camera in Barcelona. "Let's not collide with police officers, however, that's not understandable: "All of Egypt is Tahrir Square."
If the provider promises too much performance
If the Internet connection always seems much slower than advertised by the provider, there are various testing options.
Anyone who concludes an Internet contract does not have to blindly trust the speed information of the providers. Customers can even check how much of it is actually achieved in the event of abnormalities.
Finally, the pre-promised transfer rates are part of the contract and should therefore also be achieved, explains the Consumer Agency Bavaria.
If the connection always seems much slower than advertised by the provider, there are various testing options. For example, the measurement tools of the Federal Network Agency (BNetzA) measure the actual data transmission rates and compare them with the rate agreed in the contract.
"However, the tests of the providers are carried out according to provider-specific specifications and are therefore not calculated uniformly and objectively," says the consumer center Bavaria on its page.
If the connection is much slower than advertised, customers can set the provider a reasonable period of usually 14 days in order to produce the promised service. This is best done by registered in writing.
If this does not work even after multiple requests, customers can terminate and possibly also demand damages. However, in the absence of success, only the legal action will help.
First senior Republican for inquiry
As the first senior Republican, Vermont's governor has advocated an impeachment investigation against U.S. President Donald Trump. "I think the investigation is important, yes, and where that leads from here, is determined by the facts that will be determined," Phil Scott said at a news conference Thursday. He wants to know more about the whistleblower affair before action - an impeachment procedure can be taken.
Scott said he wasn't surprised by revelations that Trump had repeatedly urged Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky in a phone phone call to investigate interventions by Democratic presidential candidate and former Vice President Joe Biden in Ukraine. Because Trump has been observing Biden "for years."
Republicans have so far refrained with criticism of Trump over the whistleblower affair; few have revealed unequencies about it. Scott's remarks are a first sign of this in Trump's party. Scott, a moderate Republican, has already opposed Trump's policies on several occasions, such as easing environmental regulations and tightening immigration regulations.
Spider-Man stays at MCU: Another Marvel movie planned
It was a shock for all Spider-Man fans: At the end of August, it was announced that Sony and Marvel will go their separate ways in the future. Tom Holland's Spider-Man would therefore no longer be part of the "Avengers" universe. It was completely unclear how it should proceed with the films around Tom Holland.
Now the surprise: Spider-Man receives after "Spider-Man: Homecoming" and "Spider-Man: Far From Home" another final third film within the Marvel Cinematic Universe (MCU). Several U.S. media reports. According to this, the theatrical release should already be determined: In the USA, the film is to start from July 16, 2021.
"I'm thrilled that Spidey's path will continue in the MCU. I and everyone at the Marvel studios are very pleased that we can keep working on it ... He's the only superhero with the ability to play in different universes as Sony continues to work on his Spidey verse. You never know what the future will bring."
But how comes the sudden good news? Allegedly, Sony Marvel is said to have submitted an offere. According to industry magazines, Disney, which also includes Marvel, would participate with 30 percent in the revenues of upcoming Spider-Man films. However, the news is not confirmed.
This is how Duchess Meghan and Prince Harry show up in 'Suits' finale
After nine seasons, the series "Suits" in the USA has now reached the grand finale. In the last episode, there were many happy endings and just as many cameo appearances. And also the royal couple Prince Harry (35) and his Meghan (38), who participated in the series for many years, tributed in a charming way. Attention, the following sections include spoilers for the series.
One thing first: Duchess Meghan didn't return in the finale for a final farewal greeting as Rachel Zane, at least not for new scenes. Instead, some hindsight once again showed the most memorable scenes with today's Duchess. For example, the moments of the failed and successful wedding with Mike or how she greets him after his imprisonment.
However, even her real husband, Prince Harry, was casually mentioned in the "Suits" finale, of course, in a wedding scene. When Louis Litt (Rick Hoffman, 49) steps straight towards the altar to marry his fiancee Sheila (Rachael Harris, 51), Harvey Specter (Gabriel Macht, 47) makes a telling remark about his suit.
Aegean: Baby and four other children drown in boat crash
During the capsizing of a refugee boat in the Aegean, a baby and four other children drowned. In total, seven people died in the accident, as the Greek coast guard announced on Friday. The nationalities are still unclear.
The boat sank near the Greek island of Inousses, located between the island of Chios and Turkey's mainland. Four children, three women and five men could be rescued.
Hundreds of migrants are currently defecting from Turkey to the Greek islands in the Aegean. Last year, 174 people were killed on this route, according to the United Nations Refugee Agency (UNHCR). Interior Minister Horst Seehofer has now announced that they want to visit both countries.
Together with his French colleague and the responsible EU commissioner, he wants to "inform himself in Turkey about how it stands for the refugee situation," Seehofer said on Friday. Subsequently, he wanted to learn more about the "administrative problems of the Greeks" in Athens in order to be able to help cope.
A spokeswoman for the EU Commission confirmed Seehofer's planned trip on Friday with EU Interior Commissioner Dimitris Avramopoulos and France's Interior Minister Christophe Castaner.
An unusual number of migrants continue to arrive in Greece. On Tuesday alone, 654 people transferred to the Greek islands, the Greek civic protection ministry said on Wednesday.
Is the Turkey deal still working?
The increased figures recently raised doubts about the functioning of the EU-Turkey agreement concluded in 2016. This stipulates that the EU can send back all refugees and migrants who come illegally via Turkey to the Greek Islands. In return, the EU wanted to accept a Syrian refugee from Turkey for every deported Syrian.
However, the processing of asylum applications is progressing on the Greek side due to a lack of staffiles. Lesbos, Chios, Samos, Leros and Kos, according to the Ministry of Civil Protection, almost 29 400 people are waiting. The worst is the situation on Lesvos: 5200 migrants are currently accommodated in a camp with capacities for 650 people.
Never since the EU-Turkey Refugee Pact came into force in March 2016 have there been so many.
Climate change: Heat leads to more heart attacks
According to physicians, climate change will put the health system to the test in the future. Climate changes are "quite certainly" one of the biggest challenges for the health system in the next 20 to 50 years, said Andreas Zeiher, president of the German Society of Cardiology. Above all, the cardiovascular system made high temperatures and large temperature jumps within a short time to create and increasingly led to heart attacks.
Greens politician must answer for embarrassing photo breakdown
The leader of the Canadian Greens, Elizabeth May, in the election campaign - for this reason the cup was manipulated?
The head of the Green Party holds a reusable cup in her hand in a photo. But now it came out: The cup was manipulated.
Ottawa/Canada - It has gone unnoticed for quite quite a long time - and finally noticed. The reusable coffee mug with metal straw Elizabeth May, the head of the Canadian Greens, has never held in her hand. On the party's website, she could be seen in a photo with this mug, in conversation with citizens. However, the photo had already been published and also spread to the media - and there was still a po
Greens leader has to answer for photo breakdown
The National Post then accused May of manipulation and that the party was producing fake news in order to appear more credible on the issue of "environmental protection". And this in the middle of an election campaign for the lower house election, in which it is also increasingly difficult for Trudeau because of a bribery scandal.
The photo went viral on social media, fueling the discussion about fake news. One user shares a juxtaposition of the photos and writes: "The Greens are manipulating a photo of their chairers to hide their disposable cup."
May was shocked and asserted on her blog that she knew nothing about this action. To underline this, she immediately posted the original: She, in conversation with citizens, a cup in her hand. However, she put it correctly, was made of bamboo. A spokeswoman for the party said that it was only a matter of the party logo in the picture.
"I don't want to call him stupid, but there's nothing to hide," she tells the Guardian and at the same time asked for something from her over-engaged team: "If they want to edit a picture again, couldn't do something with my face, so I look younger?"
The German Greens have also fallen into shitstorms because of their use of single-use plastic or because of air travel - but their pictures weren't photo-photo-shopped. The head of the Bavarian Greens, Katharina Schulze, ate ice with plastic spoons on holiday, as merkur.de* reported.
Influencer wants free upgrade: Lifelong flight ban at Cathay
Jaqueline Ng was supposedly granted an upgrade by Cathay Pacific via email, but when she asked at check-in, she wasn’t allowed to board – and she’ll never be allowed to fly with Cathay again.
Spontaneously getting an upgrade for business class - that's what many passengers dream of. Sometimes they're lucky, and the employees at check-in offer a change from economy class for free.
However, this case shows how not to do it: A self-declared influencer has actively asked the airline Cathay Pacific for a free upgrade. Jaqueline Ng more than 65,000 users follow on Instagram. On May 30, she flew from Taiwan to New York and on June 7.
On the outbound flight, the employees refused Jaqueline an upgrade, although she presented an e-mail in which Cathay Pacific allegedly offered an upgrade. The influencer wants to have received the airline's influencer. The airline, on the other hand, accuses Jaqueline of falsifying the mail.
On the return flight, Jaqueline tried again to get an upgrade for the long-haul flight. This time the woman is said to have shown the same mail, but with a different reference number. The employees at JFK airport were skeptical: They checked at the headquarters. And in fact, the employee, from whom the e-mail allegedly originated, disputed the e-mail contact with Jaqueline. This was also confirmed by the mailing system.
The airline staff at the airport canceled Jaqueline's ticket and imposed a lifetime flight ban with Cathay Pacific. For $1,400 (around 1,280 euros), she had to buy herself a new flight tick to Taiwan.
With her case, the influencer turned to Elliot Advocacy, a company that represents consumer interests. Cathay had reimbursed her the price for her original ticket, but Jaqueline also demanded the cost of the neighbour.
But as long as Jaqueline does not submit the metadata of the mail, the case remains clear: For Cathay, the influencer has committed fraud, she remains blocked for future flights. The terms and conditions of the airline also state that it does not have to pay the new flight. Jaqueline does not want to be satisfied with it - and is now turning on a lawyer.
Oil prices give way after report on Yemen ceasefire
Oil prices fell by more than a dollar within noon on Friday. Most recently, a barrel (159 liters) of the North Sea variety Brent cost $61.83 - about 1.4 percent less than the previous day. The price of a barrel of the American variety West Texas Intermediate (WTI) fell 1.3 percent to $55.77.
The trigger for the price slide was a report by the Wall Street Journal on a trucency in Yemen. Saudi Arabia is said to have agreed to a ceasefire in four areas.
A drone attack on Saudi Arabia's oil facilities in mid-September had brought five percent of the world's oil production to a halt. The Houthi rebels allied with Iran in the civil war-torn country of Yemen had reclaimed the attacks for themselves. Saudi Arabia, like the U.S. government, blames Iran. A ceasefire would defuse political tension in the region.
Should an agreement actually be reached, one could focus on growth and demand worries, said Ole Hansen, head of raw materials strategy at Saxo Bank to the Bloomberg news agency.
Oil prices are thus continuing its downward movement in recent days. A week ago, a barrel of WTI oil still cost up to almost $59, Brent almost $65.
Most recently, Bloomberg had reported, citinging insiders, that the repair work on oil facilities in Saudi Arabia is progressing faster after the attack in mid-Sepeber, according to which the production volume has reached a volume of eight million barrels per day and is again moving towards the original quantity.
Recently, there have been several press releases, citing insiders reporting rapid progress by the state-owned oil company Saudi Aramco in the repair work, but raw material experts pointed out that Saudi Aramco is currently planning an IPO and avoiding negative headlines.
At the same time, there was some relaxation in the conflict between Iran and Western countries: The British oil tanker "Stena Impero" is more than two months after being arrested by Iran and the recently granted release on the way to leave the port. The crew is now preparing the departure while the ship continues to anchor, shipping company chief Erik Hånell told the Swedish broadcaster in a text message on Friday morning.
However, the U.S. announced it would move defense missiles and soldiers to the Iranian arch-enemy to Saudi Arabia. Fears of an escalation between the Islamic Republic and countries such as the U.S. and the U.K. had at times pushed up oil prices significantly in recent weeks.
Allegations against Trump intensify
In the affair about possible abuses of power by U.S. President Donald Trump, cover-up accusations against the White House have become loud. Several leading government employees had made an intense effort to prevent after a contentious phone call between Trump and Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky at the end of July an access to the wording of the conversation. This was stated by an anonymous whistleblower. The speaker of the House of Representatives, the Democrat Nancy Pelosi.
North Korea Considers Third Summit of Trump and Kim Questionable
North Korea has raised doubts that there will soon be a third summit of powerbroker Kim Jong-un and U.S. President Donald Trump. The sense and prospects of success of such a meeting are questionable because of a lack of progress in implementing agreements of the first summit in Singapore, top diplomat Kim Kye-gwan said on Friday, according to the KCNA agency.
The U.S. has so far done nothing to implement the decisions of the summit in the previous year, said the adviser of the North Korean Foreign Ministry. He also criticized intensified U.S. sanctions and the joint military maneuvers of the U.S. and South Korea. He hoped, however, a "wise" and "courageful" decision by Trump, so that it comes to a rapprochement again.
Trump had said on Monday that he first wanted to know what a third summit with Kim should bring before agreeing to do so. Negotiations on a disarmament of North Korea's North Korean nuclear and missile program have stalled since the failed second summit of Trump and Kim in Hani Hanoi. In June, however, both then met in the demilitarized zonation between the two Korean states so far.
